rtifacts:\n\n* repeated prompts instructing nearly identical reverification\n* repeated seat/layout confirmations\n* repeated static assertions after no corresponding architectural change\n* repeated handoff ceremony for the same narrow edge case\n* claims that broader DevPad optimization/build was progressing\n* treating Rookie planning as delivered state\n\nThese may retain historical value in the postmortem, but not as product/canonical state.\n\n⸻\n\nUNKNOWN\n\n* final live status of the capture-phase right-click implementation\n* whether accumulated renderer changes should all survive a later cleanup\n* exact amount of elapsed time attributable to each individual pass\n\n⸻\n\nN. ROOT-CAUSE CANDIDATES\n\n1. Coda orchestration drift — VERY STRONG evidence\n\nStrongest candidate.\n\nLocal findings repeatedly became new mandatory slices.\n\nCoda failed to preserve the ratio between issue severity and process cost.\n\n⸻\n\n2. Unclear stop condition — VERY STRONG evidence\n\nThere was no effective rule saying:\n\nkeyboard path works → right-click can defer.\n\nNo retry/time ceiling existed.\n\n⸻\n\n3. Overslicing — VERY STRONG evidence\n\nOne maintenance bug was decomposed into too many independent implementation/verification gates.\n\n⸻\n\n4. Missing session framing — STRONG evidence\n\nThe original ask was clear but not preserved in a durable session frame.\n\nIndividual seat prompts had scope.\n\nThe whole session did not maintain a stable outcome ledger.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Verifier recursion — STRONG but secondary evidence\n\nVerification repeatedly triggered fresh implementation.\n\nLuce itself was mostly behaving correctly.\n\nThe recursion occurred because orchestration treated every verifier finding as a reason to continue.\n\n⸻\n\n6. Builder interruption — MODERATE evidence\n\nNico completed individual patches, so literal interruption was not the primary issue.\n\nBut coherent end-to-end delivery was repeatedly broken apart by gates.\n\n⸻\n\n7. Project/context overloading — MODERATE evidence\n\nBroader discussions around Rookie/myDon/etc. blurred the perceived state of progress.\n\nBut there is weaker evidence that context overload caused the technical clipboard loop itself.\n\n⸻\n\n8. Handoff information loss — LOW evidence\n\nThe handoffs were actually detailed.\n\nThe problem was more nearly the opposite:\n\ntoo much detailed handoff/certification overhead, not missing information.\n\n⸻\n\n9. Architecture guessed before runtime measurement — VERY STRONG evidence\n\nThis is a separate technical/process root cause.\n\nThe sequence should have been:\n\nobserve event order → choose mechanism\n\nInstead it repeatedly became:\n\ninfer event order → implement mechanism → discover inference was wrong\n\nThat materially inflated the session.\n\n⸻\n\nO. EARLY WARNING SIGNALS\n\nSignal 1 — 9/10 green, but another full build cycle starts\n\nThis was the clearest early circuit-breaker.\n\nOnce Luce reported almost everything green and only right-click copy failing, someone should have asked:\n\nDoes this remaining behavior justify another cross-seat implementation cycle?\n\nThat question was not asked strongly enough.\n\n⸻\n\nSignal 2 — “one more bounded pass”\n\nThe session repeatedly used the logic:\n\nthis is now precisely isolated, so one more pass should close it.\n\nThat phrase pattern is dangerous when repeated.\n\nBy the second occurrence, the session should have been reviewed globally.\n\n⸻\n\nSignal 3 — verification without a new visible artifact\n\nMultiple verification/build cycles occurred with no new thing for Don to see.\n\nThat should have immediately triggered:\n\nStop. Show runnable outcome before continuing.\n\n⸻\n\nSignal 4 — architecture/event-model discussion inside a maintenance task\n\nOnce the conversation was about:\n\n* event capture phases\n* onSelectionChange\n* TTL caches\n* no-op mousemoves\n* selection lifecycle timing\n\nthe maintenance task had clearly escaped its expected complexity.\n\nAt that point a