anged,\n[L503] - repeatedly restating that totals remained conflict after no recalibration,\n[L504] - repeatedly restating registration=0 across several ECQ handoffs,\n[L505] - repeatedly reasserting no clock reads / one hash primitive after already verified.\n\n[L507] These are not wrong, but they are where verification began replacing progress.\n\n[L509] ## Findings that triggered architectural reconsideration\n\n[L511] The most consequential example:\n\n[L513] - a real materialization identity defect led not only to the direct hash fix, but then into envelope-version discussion, canonical transform work, shell seam work, registration posture, and another full verification chain.\n\n[L515] Those may all be valid ECQ issues, but they were **architecturally broader than the original defect**.\n\n[L517] ## Where verification became recursion\n\n[L519] Luce itself mostly behaved correctly: it found real defects and reported bounded evidence.\n\n[L521] The recursion came from orchestration:\n\n[L523] > Luce finding → Coda reframing → new Nico slice → new Luce verification → Chiave gate → new Nico seam slice → new Luce verification.\n\n[L525] The verifier was not the root cause. The orchestration repeatedly converted findings into broader gates.\n\n---\n\n[L529] # J. VISIBLE OUTCOME TEST\n\n[L531] ### After Falcon freeze\n\n[L533] Don had a visible usable thing: **today’s frozen slate package**.\n\n[L535] ### After the experimental engine run\n\n[L537] Don had a visible usable thing: **full 12-game scorecard / zero-candidate outcome**.\n\n[L539] ### After Kimi r7\n\n[L541] Don had a visible usable thing: **corrected engine state with four legitimate reviews and no candidates**.\n\n[L543] ### After Luce r7 verification\n\n[L545] Don had a visible usable thing: **independently verified accepted experimental baseline**.\n\n[L547] At this point the user-facing outcome was already strong.\n\n[L549] ### During totals calibration research\n\n[L551] Visible outcome became weaker. The work was useful, but there was no new runnable card or system behavior.\n\n[L553] ### During ECQ identity/seam work\n\n[L555] For this chat’s original purpose, Don had **no new visible MLB outcome at all**. Hours of process could accumulate while the original task was already done.\n\n[L557] ### During DevPad static verification\n\n[L559] Again, no MLB outcome. Even within DevPad, final behavioral validation was still pending.\n\n[L561] This is exactly where process overtook runnable progress.\n\n---\n\n[L565] # K. SESSION FRAME HEALTH\n\n[L567] ### Real session header?\n\n[L569] **No strong persistent header governed the whole chat.**\n\n[L571] The chat had local labels like “MLB Experimental Lane,” but no durable session-level frame that prevented unrelated project work from entering.\n\n[L573] ### Task explicitly bounded?\n\n[L575] Initially, partially. The MLB cleanup and Falcon prompts were well bounded.\n\n[L577] Later, no. The chat accepted ECQ and DevPad tasks without re-framing.\n\n[L579] ### Authority clear?\n\n[L581] Mostly yes. Don remained final authority, and seat roles were generally understood.\n\n[L583] ### Expected artifact clear?\n\n[L585] Early: yes.\n[L586] Later: increasingly no.\n\n[L588] Examples:\n\n[L590] - after r7 verification, was the desired endpoint “accepted baseline,” “new scorecard,” “totals model,” or “full methodology corpus”?\n[L591] - after ECQ FIX 1, was the endpoint hash correctness, envelope policy, full seam closure, or registration readiness?\n\n[L593] The expected artifact kept expanding.\n\n[L595] ### Stop condition?\n\nWeak.\n\n[L599] The strongest stop condition appeared accidentally when Kimi credits ran out.\n\n[L601] ### Session footer / outcome ledger?\n\n[L603] Not consistently.\n\n[L605] There were many local statuses, but no single session footer that said:\n\n[L607] > Original ask complete / these items deferred / do not continue in this thread.\n\n[L609] ### When controls disappeared\n\n[L611] The controls began degrading after the first successful engine run and were effectively gone once ECQ and DevPad work entered the same thread.\n\n---\n\n[L615] # L. WHAT SHOULD HAVE HAPPENED\n\n[L617] For the MLB task, the shortest sane path was:\n\n[L619] **MLB cleanup recon**\n[L620] → review known risks\n[L621] → **Falcon frozen pregame package**\n[L622] → **one engine run**\n[L623] → inspect scorecard\n[L624] → fix only proven blocking implementation defects\n[L625] → **one independent verification pass**\n[L626] → freeze accepted experimental baseline\n[L627] → close session.\n\n[L629] Totals calibration should have been opened as a **separate research session** because it required:\n\n[L631] - a corpus,\n[L632] - morning-after grading,\n[L633] - future scored games,\n[L634] - out-of-sample evaluation.\n\n[L636] ECQ materialization work should have lived in its own thread.\n\n[L638] DevPad clipboard work should have lived in its own thread.\n\n[L640] The key correction is not “fewer tests.” It is:\n\n[L642] > **testing inside coherent passes, rather than turning every finding into a new conversational operating frame.**\n\n---\n\n[L646] # M. SALVAGE\n\n[L648] ## KEEP\n\n[L650] - MLB estate recon.\n[L651] - Falcon 09-24 frozen package.\n[L652] - Experimental engine full-grid run.\n[L653] - odds-consumption perturbation evidence.\n[L654] - r7 run-line fail-closed behavior.\n[L655] - juice-gate single-state fix.\n[L656] - `total` ingestion normalization.\n[L657] - Luce r7 verification.\n[L658] - totals compression diagnosis.\n[L659] - 14-game corpus inventory.\n[L660] - ECQ assertion-content hash fix.\n[L661] - ECQ seam implementation.\n[L662] - DevPad served-build/static verification evidence.\n\n[L664] ## VERIFY\n\n[L666] - totals methodology scaling conclusions beyond the diagnosed compression.\n[L667] - any claim that F1 should be exactly 5–6× larger.\n[L668] - any park-factor conversion proposed from the tiny corpus.\n[L669] - ECQ selection-target codec end-to-end beha