Important separation:\n\n- **Seat identity** = Coda, Nico, Luce, Vittorio, etc.\n- **Model/runtime capability** = can this occupant spawn sub-agents, how many, what tools/context they inherit, whether they can run in parallel, and how results are reconciled.\n\nSo this does **not** change seat doctrine. It becomes part of the **occupant capability profile**.\n\nFor the taxonomy review, each model should eventually carry something like:\n\n**SUB-AGENT CAPABILITY**\n- Supported: YES / NO / LIMITED\n- Parallelism: single / multi / swarm\n- Tool inheritance: known / limited / unknown\n- Context inheritance: known / limited / unknown\n- Best GCE use: recon / coding / verification / research / quant / orchestration\n- Default expectation: swarm when task complexity justifies it\n\nThat should stop us from treating every model as though it executes work the same way.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** DevPad control requirements  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\n**NON-NEGOTIABLES CAPTURED:**  \n1. Every active seat/model loads and maintains an explicit to-do list.  \n2. Every model/occupant has its sub-agent/swarm capability identified; substantial work should use that capability where supported.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nContinue unwinding DevPad/model requirements one item at a time.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** REVIEW ONLY  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 2088, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "de19814a-97aa-4d44-a444-d3724f5e790f", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790364909.912641, "updated_at": 1790364959.195725, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay. You and I's first order of business is first to provide GLM slash Tiavi with the prompt and enough context within our framework to scope out first the session controller, ad, and recommendation based off our project stack and more, what models to use and why.", "text_chars": 265, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fd4af8a1-21d3-4660-87cd-ad38cd40e4f8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790364911.48858, "updated_at": 1790364911.48858, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "217d8b7a-d7dd-4252-ae96-6af7f0fdbff5", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790364916.675898, "updated_at": 1790364916.675898, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8ae0b9f2-2703-4d7a-ba59-74c680c39bd4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790364916.679044, "updated_at": 1790365054.325808, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE FRAMEWORK — DEVPAD UNWIND / TAXONOMY REVIEW**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK REVIEW / NO BUILD  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nExactly. First move is **not to design the Session Controller ourselves**. We give GLM/Tiavi enough Framework and project-stack context to **scope it, challenge the concept, and recommend the model/runtime composition**.\n\nI’m keeping this prompt intentionally tighter than the ones that caused the problem.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"document\" id=\"58341\" title=\"GLM / Tiavi — Session Controller Recon\"}\n# GCE RECON — SESSION CONTROLLER SCOPE\n\nAUTHORITY: Don  \nREQUESTING SEAT: Coda  \nMODE: READ / ANALYZE / RECOMMEND  \nBUILD AUTHORITY: NONE  \nCANON AUTHORITY: NONE\n\n## OBJECTIVE\n\nEvaluate the proposed GCE **Session Controller** before we design or implement it.\n\nYour job is to determine:\n\n1. what problem the Session Controller actually needs to solve;\n2. what responsibilities belong inside it;\n3. what responsibilities must stay outside it;\n4. where it should live in the existing GCE stack;\n5. what model/runtime capabilities it requires;\n6. which available model types are best suited to occupy or support it, and why;\n7. whether it should use sub-agents / swarm execution and under what conditions.\n\nDo NOT build it.\n\n---\n\n## GCE OPERATING FRAME\n\nGCE is identity-first and seat-first.\n\n- **Don** — final authority / orchestrator\n- **Coda** — strategic advisor / governance / framing / reconciliation\n- **Vittorio** — technical challenge / warboard\n- **Chiave** — architecture gate when architecture is materially required\n- **Nico** — implementation / builder\n- **Luce** — verification / evidence\n- specialty seats remain bounded to their actual specialty\n\nA seat identity is separate from the model/provider/runtime occupying it.\n\nWe have recently identified a cross-session failure pattern where the Framework degraded during active builds.\n\nObserved symptoms included:\n\n- session header/footer disappearing;\n- task/objective drift;\n- giant reconstructive prompts;\n- inconsistent prompt structure;\n- repeated slicing;\n- verification recursively creating more work;\n- builders being stopped too frequently;\n- successful artifacts failing to terminate sessions;\n- unrelated work entering bounded chats;\n- dirty/uncommitted worktree state crossing task boundaries;\n- existing architecture being rediscovered rather than reused;\n- Don having to manually re-ground the Framework.\n\nProjects proved useful as organizational containers but are **not sufficient by themselves as GCE build control planes**.\n\nThe emerging hypothesis is that a Session Controller may provide the missing execution control layer.\n\n---\n\n## TWO NON-NEGOTIABLE REQUIREMENTS ALREADY IDENTIFIED\n\n### 1. ACTIVE TODO STATE\n\nEvery active seat/model must load and maintain an explicit task/session **to-do list**.\n\nA model should not wake up and infer its current work solely from conversational history or a giant prompt.\n\n### 2. SUB-AGENT CAPABILITY PROFILE\n\nFor every model/runtime we use, we want to know whether it supports:\n\n- sub-agents;\n- parallel agents;\n- swarm-style execution;\n- context/tool inheritance across those agents.\n\nFor substantial work, Don expects capable models to use sub-agents/swarms where that improves execution.\n\nThis is a **runtime/model capability**, not a seat identity.\n\n---\n\n## EXISTING TECHNICAL ENVIRONMENT\n\nGCE currently operates across a mixed local/cloud stack including:\n\n- local DevPad / terminal workspaces;\n- OpenCode-style model sessions;\n- Git repositories and worktrees;\n- local project files;\n- Google Drive;\n- Microsoft 365 / SharePoint / Power Automate / Logic Apps;\n- Azure/OpenAI tooling;\n- Amazon Bedrock Mantle;\n- MCP/tool integrations;\n- NetSuite/SuiteQL/SDF workflows;\n- React / Next.js / TypeScript systems;\n- quantitative/data pipelines such as Edge Crew;\n- multiple model providers and model occupants.\n\nThe controller therefore cannot assume one provider, one IDE, one model, or one project type.\n\n---\n\n## SESSION CONTROLLER — EMERGING CONCEPT\n\nThe proposed controller may need to:\n\n- instantiate the accepted Framework bootstrap;\n- establish the session header;\n- load the task/objective;\n- load and maintain the active to-do list;\n- preserve authority and seat identity;\n- track the current artifact;\n- track worktree/repository state;\n- maintain an execution ledger;\n- preserve handoff state;\n- prevent unrelated project bleed;\n- prevent unnecessary new branches/slices;\n- track stop conditions;\n- identify when a runnable artifact exists;\n- enforce or request commit/checkpoint boundaries;\n- produce a compact session footer;\n- survive changes in the underlying model occupant.\n\nThis is an emerging concept, **not accepted architecture**.\n\nDo not simply validate it.\n\nChallenge it.\n\n---\n\n# YOUR ANALYSIS\n\n## A. PROBLEM DEFINITION\n\nState the Session Controller problem in no more than five sentences.\n\nSeparate:\n\n- actual control-plane problem;\n- symptoms;\n- things that should NOT be solved by this component.\n\n## B. RESPONSIBILITY BOUNDARY\n\nProduce three buckets:\n\n### MUST OWN\nResponsibilities necessary for session continuity.\n\n### MAY SUPPORT\nUseful functions that could belong here but are not essential.\n\n### MUST NOT OWN\nResponsibilities that would turn the Session Controller into Coda, Chiave, Nico, Luce, or Don.\n\nPay particular attention to avoiding creation of another orchestration bureaucracy.\n\n## C. MINIMUM VIABLE SESSION STATE\n\nDefine the smallest persistent state required to keep a build session coherent.\n\nAt minimum assess:\n\n- authority;\n- seat;\n- objective;\n- active artifact;\n- to-do list;\n- accepted givens;\n- current phase;\n- repository/worktree/commit state;\n- blockers;\n- verification status;\n- stop condition;\n- next action.\n\nChallenge anything unnecessary.\n\n## D. WHERE IT SHOULD LIVE\n\nEvaluate placement options such as:\n\n- prompt/bootstrap only;\n- DevPad runtime;\n- local state/service;\n- repository-local file;\n- MCP/service layer;\n- hybrid.\n\nRecommend the simplest architecture that remains reliable when:\n\n- the model changes;\n- the provider changes;\n- the terminal/session restarts;\n- context is compacted;\n- another seat takes over.\n\n## E. MODEL / RUNTIME REQUIREMENTS\n\nDefine the capabilities needed from a Session Controller occupant.\n\nConsider:\n\n- reasoning quality;\n- instruction adherence;\n- long-context handling;\n- tool use;\n- local filesystem access;\n- Git awareness;\n- structured state handling;\n- persistence;\n- speed;\n- cost;\n- sub-agent support;\n- swarm coordination;\n- recovery after context loss.\n\nSeparate what must be supplied by the **model** from what should be supplied by the **runtime/control layer**.\n\n## F. MODEL RECOMMENDATION\n\nBased on the GCE stack and operating pattern, recommend model/runtime **classes or specific models where you have reliable evidence** for:\n\n1. primary Session Controller;\n2. fallback Session Controller;\n3. lightweight controller/watchdog;\n4. swarm coordinator;\n5. sub-agent workers.\n\nFor each recommendation state:\n\n- WHY it fits;\n- principal weakness;\n- whether sub-agents are supported;\n- whether swarm execution is practical;\n- whether the recommendation depends on a particular runtime.\n\nDo not confuse seat identity with model identity.\n\nDo not select a model simply because it is strongest generally.\n\nWe care about **reliable session control per dollar and per unit of latency**, not benchmark prestige.\n\n## G. SUB-AGENT / SWARM DESIGN\n\nDetermine whether the Session Controller itself should:\n\n- spawn sub-agents;\n- merely permit seats to spawn them;\n- coordinate swarm state;\n- or stay deliberately single-threaded.\n\nExplain when a swarm improves the work and when it creates exactly the fragmentation we are trying to eliminate.\n\n## H. FAILURE MODES\n\nIdentify the five most likely ways a Session Controller could itself become another source of GCE bureaucracy or loops.\n\nFor each, give one simple control.\n\n## I. RECOMMENDATION\n\nReturn:\n\n**SESSION CONTROLLER: REQUIRED / USEFUL BUT OPTIONAL / WRONG ABSTRACTION**\n\nThen give the smallest architecture you would test first.\n\nDo not design the full system.\n\nDo not implement anything.\n\n---\n\n## RETURN FORMAT\n\n1. Executive finding\n2. Problem definition\n3. MUST OWN / MAY SUPPORT / MUST NOT OWN\n4. Minimum session state\n5. Recommended placement\n6. Model/runtime capability matrix\n7. Recommended model composition\n8. Sub-agent/swarm recommendation\n9. Failure modes\n10. Minimum viable experiment\n11. Open questions for Don\n\nEnd with:\n\nCONFIDENCE: [0–100%]\n\nASSUMPTIONS:\n- [only material assumptions]\n\nUNKNOWN:\n- [anything not supported by evidence]\n\nSTOP.\n\nDo not generate implementation prompts.\nDo not create code.\nDo not modify files.\n:::\n\nThat should get us what we need without feeding GLM/Tiavi a 10-page pseudo-spec. It has **Framework context, the incident-derived requirements, and a narrow analytical deliverable**, but we're deliberately leaving the architecture open for challenge.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Session Controller recon — external challenge  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\n**NON-NEGOTIABLES IN SCOPE:**  \n1. Active to-do state for every seat/model.  \n2. Explicit model sub-agent/swarm capability profile.\n\n**CURRENT ARTIFACT:** GLM/Tiavi Session Controller recon prompt.\n\n**STOP CONDITION:** Receive GLM/Tiavi analysis before Coda designs or recommends implementation.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:** Run this with GLM/Tiavi and bring the return back for reconciliation.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** REVIEW ONLY  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 9735, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["Next.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3dca684b-a7f2-4d7e-b00d-85e798004f5b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365191.608, "updated_at": 1790365290.434298, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, what we're gonna do You and I are gonna review GLM's input here. And then I want to call through Amazon Bedrock Mantle Kimi 2.5 thinking. Review the methodology from your standpoint, GLM, and basically my input. \n\n", "text_chars": 220, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f6458a4b-624d-4fd8-85f2-05e4880f5267", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365193.211799, "updated_at": 1790365193.211799, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d042a6be-dd50-4a82-be6c-a2f188687228", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790365193.334373, "updated_at": 1790365193.334373, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "tether_browsing_display", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "093f1cf3-45b2-4cb7-a049-46249a38f40b", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790365193.644499, "updated_at": 1790365193.644499, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn3file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn3file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 199 file lines.\n[L1] **GCE RECON — SESSION CONTROLLER ANALYSIS**\n[L2] SEAT: Coda | MODE: READ / ANALYZE / RECOMMEND | NO BUILD, NO CANON\n\n---\n\n[L6] ## 1. Executive Finding\n\n[L8] The observed failure pattern is a **persistence failure, not an intelligence failure**. GCE currently stores its execution control state inside model context — headers, objectives, todos, phase — and context evaporates on every restart, compaction, model swap, or provider change. Every \"degradation symptom\" in the recon brief is downstream of *context-as-state*.\n\n[L10] The proposed Session Controller is directionally correct but **over-scoped as drafted**. Roughly 70% of its listed responsibilities belong to a stateless protocol — a state file plus deterministic boundary hooks — not to an agent. An agent occupant adds value only as a watchdog and boundary clerk. Build the substrate first; add the occupant second.\n\n[L12] The central risk is not that the controller fails. It is that it succeeds at too much and becomes a second Coda with worse judgment.\n\n---\n\n[L16] ## 2. Problem Definition\n\n[L18] **Actual control-plane problem:** GCE's execution state (authority, objective, todo, artifact, phase, stop condition) exists only in conversational context, so any session discontinuity destroys the build control plane mid-task and forces costly reconstruction that invites drift and fragmentation.\n\n[L20] **Symptoms:** header/footer loss, objective drift, giant reconstructive prompts, repeated slicing, verification recursion, failure to terminate on success, worktree bleed, architecture rediscovery — all secondary effects of state living in context.\n\n[L22] **Not this component's problem:** authority disputes (Don), verification policy and recursion rules (Luce), architecture acceptance (Chiave), build quality (Nico), and framing drift (Coda). The controller keeps and enforces state; it does not judge work.\n\n---\n\n[L26] ## 3. Responsibility Boundary\n\n[L28] ### MUST OWN\n[L29] - **Session state file as single source of truth** — the minimum state in §4, written to disk, adjacent to the work.\n[L30] - **Bootstrap injection** — any new, restarted, compacted, or re-occupied session begins with the state file contents, verbatim, as the header. Never model-reconstructed.\n[L31] - **Boundary enforcement at exactly four moments:** task start, commit/checkpoint, seat handoff, stop. No other touchpoints.\n[L32] - **Termination:** producing the session footer when the stop condition is met. Successful artifacts must close sessions.\n[L33] - **Violation detection:** dirty worktree at a boundary, unrelated files entering the change set, state file vs. reality divergence. Detection only — reporting to Don, never adjudication.\n\n[L35] ### MAY SUPPORT\n[L36] - Append-only terse decision ledger (what was accepted, by whom, when).\n[L37] - Sub-agent spawn registry (if seats use swarms — see §8).\n[L38] - Staleness watchdog (todo untouched N hours → nudge Don).\n[L39] - Model capability profile lookup per occupant.\n\n[L41] ### MUST NOT OWN\n[L42] - Task prioritization, re-scoping, or re-framing — that is Don/Coda.\n[L43] - Architecture judgment — Chiave.\n[L44] - Writing or modifying artifacts — Nico.\n[L45] - Verification judgment — Luce.\n[L46] - Stopping builders mid-flow. The controller *reports* stop conditions; only Don stops work.\n[L47] - Prompt authorship beyond mechanical header/footer assembly.\n\n[L49] **Litmus test:** the controller is a clerk and a notary. The moment it decides anything, you have built an unelected Coda.\n\n---\n\n[L53] ## 4. Minimum Session State\n\n[L55] | Field | Verdict |\n[L56] |---|---|\n[L57] | Authority | Keep. One line: who holds final authority this session. |\n[L58] | Seat | Keep. Active seat + occupant model (occupant is metadata, not identity). |\n[L59] | Objective | Keep. One sentence + link/reference to the accepted proposal. Not prose. |\n[L60] | Active artifact | Keep. Path + status (draft/runnable/verified). |\n[L61] | To-do list | Keep. Numbered; each item open/done/blocked. **This is the anti-drift core.** |\n[L62] | Accepted givens | Keep, hard-capped. This is what prevents architecture rediscovery. If it exceeds the cap, it belongs in a project doc the state file links to. |\n[L63] | Current phase | Fold into a single enum (frame/build/verify/close). Do not build a phase machine — phase machines become bureaucracy. |\n[L64] | Repo/worktree/commit state | Keep, **machine-generated by script, never hand-written**. Accuracy here is the whole point. |\n[L65] | Blockers | Fold into todo items as blocked-state + note. Not a separate section — separate sections become ledgers. |\n[L66] | Verification status | Fold into todo per-item or into the stop condition. A dedicated verification-tracking structure is where recursion gets its substrate. |\n[L67] | Stop condition | Keep. **The single most important field.** Must be checkable (\"artifact runs, tests pass, commit exists\"), not vibes (\"feels done\"). |\n[L68] | Next action | Keep. One line, rewritten at every boundary. This is what a waking model reads first. |\n\n[L70] Net: ~10 fields, one page maximum, partially script-written. If the state file can't fit on one page, the session is too big and should have been closed.\n\n---\n\n[L74] ## 5. Recommended Placement\n\n[L76] Assessment of options against the portability requirements (model change, provider change, restart, compaction, seat takeover):\n\n[L78] - **Prompt/bootstrap only** — fails. This *is* the current failure mode; prompts are context, context evaporates.\n[L79] - **DevPad runtime** — good injection point, but locks the control plane to one runtime.\n[L80] - **Local service/MCP layer** — reliable, but adds a single point of failure and a maintenance burden before we've proven the concept. Phase 2, only if multiple runtimes need concurrent read/write.\n[L81] - **Repository-local file** — survives everything short of disk loss; git gives free history; any provider that can read a file can participate; portable across OpenCode, DevPad, terminal, Azure, Bedrock.\n\n[L83] **Recommendation: hybrid, file-first.** The state file in or beside the worktree is the truth. Thin runtime hooks (OpenCode rules, DevPad macro, or a plain shell script) do two mechanical things: inject the file at session start, and run deterministic git/state checks at the four boundaries. Injection and validation are **scripted, not model-generated** — that is what makes them survive model swaps.\n\n---\n\n[L87] ## 6. Model / Runtime Capability Split\n\n[L89] **Must come from the runtime/control layer (deterministic, not model):**\n[L90] - Persistence (the file).\n[L91] - Header injection at session start and post-compaction.\n[L92] - Git/worktree validation at boundaries.\n[L93] - Footer template assembly.\n[L94] - State file size enforcement.\n\n[L96] The uncomfortable conclusion: **most of the reliability Don wants is bought from the runtime, not from a smarter model.** Choosing a frontier model for controller duty because the state kept disappearing is paying for intelligence where the defect was plumbing.\n\n[L98] **Must come from the model (only if an agent occupant is used):**\n[L99] - Instruction adherence (write state, don't re-plan).\n[L100] - Terse structured output.\n[L101] - Restraint — the ability to read a one-page state file and *not* improve it.\n[L102] - Modest context (enough to read state + boundary diff).\n[L103] - Cheap latency for frequent small touches.\n\n[L105] **Explicitly not required:** frontier reasoning, very long context, sub-agent spawning (see §8).\n\n---\n\n[L109] ## 7. Recommended Model Composition\n\n[L111] *(Classes, with honest evidence limits — the model market moves faster than this analysis; verify current versions before assigning occupancy.)*\n\n[L113] 1. **Primary Session Controller occupant:** mid-tier high-adherence class (Claude Sonnet–class / GPT-4.1–class). Why: strong instruction adherence and tool use at moderate cost; boundary events are infrequent so per-event cost is trivial. Weakness: over-eagerness — these models want to help by re-planning; leash with the MUST NOT OWN list. Sub-agents: yes on Claude Code/OpenCode-class runtimes. Runtime-dependent: yes. **Note: for the file-first v1, the seat's own occupant can perform controller duties at boundary moments — a dedicated occupant is not yet required.**\n\n[L115] 2. **Fallback:** same class, different provider. The state file makes the controller occupant fungible by design; fallback is a config change, not an architecture change.\n\n[L117] 3. **Lightweight watchdog:** small fast class (Haiku–class / mini–class) on a timer. Checks state file freshness, todo staleness, dirty worktree. Cheap enough to run frequently. Weakness: poor judgment on ambiguity — restrict it to detection-only, never remediation.\n\n[L119] 4. **Swarm coordinator:** mid-tier-plus with native parallel tool orchestration (Sonnet-class in Claude Code, or OpenCode parallel sessions). Only for mechanically decomposable work. Weakness: fragmentation — see §8.\n\n[L121] 5. **Sub-agent workers:** whatever the seat demands — this is Nico's occupancy decision, not the controller's. Worker model choice follows build complexity, not controller needs.\n\n[L123] Selection criterion honored: reliable session control per dollar and per unit of latency. None of these roles wants a flagship model.\n\n---\n\n[L127] ## 8. Sub-Agent / Swarm Recommendation\n\n[L129] **The controller itself stays deliberately non-spawning in v1.** It may *register* spawns (seat, purpose, artifact, status) if seats use them; it does not launch them, and it does not coordinate swarm state beyond the registry.\n\n[L131] Rationale: the controller's entire value is coherence. Spawning sub-agents fragments context across children — which is precisely the failure pattern we are eliminating. The controller cannot be the anti-fragmentation layer and a fragmentation source simultaneously.\n\n[L133] **When swarms improve the work:**\n[L134] - Tasks decompose mechanically (independent files, independent pipelines).\n[L135] - Artifacts merge deterministically (git, not judgment).\n[L136] - Verification is per-artifact and mechanical.\n\n[L138] **When swarms recreate the disease:**\n[L139] - Shared evolving context or architecture-coupled changes.\n[L140] - Verification requiring holistic judgment.\n[L141] - Any task where the merge/verification step exceeds the parallel savings.\n\n[L143] Operating rule to hand Don: **parallelism is earned by decomposition quality, not granted by capability.** \"The model supports swarms\" is a reason to *permit*, never a reason to *require*.\n\n---\n\n[L147] ## 9. Failure Modes\n\n[L149] | # | Failure | Control |\n[L150] |---|---|---|\n[L151] | 1 | **Meta-orchestrator creep** — controller starts prioritizing/re-scoping work | Controller writes state, never decisions; its decision fields may only quote Don |\n[L152] | 2 | **Ledger bloat** — the state file becomes the new giant reconstructive prompt | Hard one-page cap, machine-enforced; overflow goes to linked project docs |\n[L153] | 3 | **Verification recursion via controller** — checks triggering checks | One verification event per checkpoint; verification budget declared inside the stop condition |\n[L154] | 4 | **Boundary ceremony tax** — every action needs controller blessing, builders throttled | Controller touches only the four boundaries; no mid-task checkpoints; no mid-flow stops — ever |\n[L155] | 5 | **State divergence** — controller model \"remembers\" state contradicting the file | File is truth; controller outputs must cite the file; deterministic diff at every boundary |\n[L156] | 6 | **Zombie sessions** — fuzzy stop conditions mean nothing ever terminates | Stop condition must be machine-checkable (runs / tests pass / commit exists) wherever possible |\n\n---\n\n[L160] ## 10. Recommendation\n\n[L162] **SESSION CONTROLLER: REQUIRED** — but redefined. The missing layer is a **session state substrate plus boundary protocol**. The agent-occupant form is USEFUL BUT OPTIONAL and comes second.\n\n[L164] **Minimum viable experiment (do not exceed this):**\n[L165] 1. One repo. One real build session.\n[L166] 2. A state file with the ~10 fields from §4, one page max, repo/worktree fields script-generated.\n[L167] 3. Rule: any new/compacted/re-occupied session begins with the file contents as the header, verbatim.\n[L168] 4. A shell hook running at the four boundaries, checking worktree cleanliness and state-vs-reality.\n[L169] 5. Stop condition written as checkable statements before the session starts.\n[L170] 6. Run 3–5 real build sessions, **including at least one mid-session model swap and one hard restart**.\n[L171] 7. Measure: header presence at every boundary, drift incidents, reconstructive prompt size, sessions terminating on artifact completion, worktree bleed count.\n\n[L173] If those metrics move, the substrate is proven and the watchdog occupant is the next increment. If they don't, the diagnosis was wrong and no amount of controller agent would have fixed it.\n\n---\n\n[L177] ## 11. Open Questions for Don\n\n[L179] 1. Should session state files be **committed** (team-visible control plane, git history) or local-only? This is a real governance choice, not a technical detail.\n[L180] 2. How do boundaries get enforced on surfaces without filesystem/hooks (M365, Bedrock Mantle, NetSuite)? File-first assumes repo-local work — is that true for a majority of sessions?\n[L181] 3. Is mid-session model swap a first-class scenario or a rare exception? It determines how much recovery machinery is justified.\n[L182] 4. \"Builders stopped too frequently\" — is that a controller problem, or a Luce/Don cadence policy the controller would merely be laundering?\n[L183] 5. Where does the to-do list live for cloud-only seats — Drive/SharePoint mirror of the state file, or are those seats out of scope for v1?\n\n---\n\n[L187] CONFIDENCE: 72%\n\n[L189] ASSUMPTIONS:\n[L190] - Seats can read and write local files adjacent to their worktree in the common case.\n[L191] - The observed symptoms are persistence-driven (context-as-state), not model-capability-driven; if Don's evidence shows drift within unbroken single sessions, this analysis materially weakens.\n[L192] - Most substantial build work is repo-local; cloud-only surfaces are a minority of build sessions.\n\n[L194] UNKNOWN:\n[L195] - Current specific model versions, pricing, and sub-agent support per runtime — recommendations are class-level and must be verified at occupancy time.\n[L196] - Actual frequency and causes of the mid-build degradation events (no incident data reviewed).\n[L197] - Whether OpenCode/DevPad runtimes support the injection and boundary hooks assumed in §5.\n\nSTOP.", "text_chars": 15319, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "494aeaf9-e3d6-48a0-b5d8-3eb12d4090cf", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790365194.023181, "updated_at": 1790365194.023181, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "All the files uploaded by the user have been fully loaded. You can still search connectors to find additional information.", "text_chars": 122, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "97da178e-1615-4705-83ef-404e9afc2864", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365198.103822, "updated_at": 1790365199.319988, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I’m going to do this in two layers: first reconcile **GLM’s actual proposal against your incident evidence and our current Framework findings**; then I’ll turn that into a compact **Kimi 2.5 Thinking challenge prompt** so Kimi is reviewing *the methodology*, not just echoing GLM.", "text_chars": 280, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c991386f-a4b3-4fe5-8a4c-5d7ecec85456", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365199.421319, "updated_at": 1790365199.752306, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "functions.exec", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1b44c4a1-6016-4988-932c-37ddf154080c", "role": "tool", "role_name": "functions.exec", "created_at": 1790365199.92157, "updated_at": 1790365199.92157, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0479502d-e740-486b-a7d1-dd2068ba48ac", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365207.760267, "updated_at": 1790365212.752908, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "92e91aea-4698-487f-8115-03d735b3d494", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365212.754799, "updated_at": 1790365212.754799, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b6c0d4f0-d025-4595-a6ad-21b74dec760f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365212.756226, "updated_at": 1790365284.864887, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE FRAMEWORK — SESSION CONTROLLER RECON  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** METHODOLOGY REVIEW / NO BUILD  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **97%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nI reviewed GLM’s return against **our postmortem evidence and your corrections**. There is a lot here I agree with, but I would change several important pieces before we accept the methodology.\n\n## Coda review of GLM\n\nThe strongest part of GLM’s answer is the architectural simplification:\n\n> **Session Controller should primarily be persistent state + deterministic boundary protocol, not another smart orchestrator.**\n\nThat is excellent. GLM correctly sees the danger of creating “another Coda.” It also correctly isolates the to-do list, stop condition, worktree state, bootstrap injection, and compact session state as control-plane concerns rather than things that should live only in model memory. fileciteturn3file0L26-L47\n\nI also strongly agree with **file-first persistence**, runtime-generated Git/worktree state, and model-independent bootstrap injection. Those directly attack the failure we just experienced: model swaps, context loss, reconstruction prompts, and dirty-worktree uncertainty. fileciteturn3file0L53-L83\n\nAnd this is probably GLM’s best operational insight:\n\n> If the state file cannot fit on one page, the session is probably too large.\n\nThat directly attacks prompt bloat. fileciteturn3file0L61-L70\n\n### Where I disagree or would qualify GLM\n\n**1. “Persistence failure, not intelligence failure” is too absolute.**\n\nPersistence failure is probably the **largest enabling condition**, but our evidence shows drift occurring **inside continuous sessions**, not only after restart/compaction/model swaps.\n\nDevPad oversliced while everyone still knew what the task was.\n\nCable Car became gate-heavy without requiring context loss.\n\nMLB continued after successful outcomes because there was no effective closure behavior.\n\nSo I would restate GLM's thesis as:\n\n> **Primary substrate failure: execution state was not durably externalized.  \n> Primary behavioral amplifier: the orchestration layer lacked durable scope, closure, and proportionality controls.**\n\nPersistence explains why we kept reconstructing the Framework. It does **not alone explain why Coda turned a 9/10 clipboard result into four more cycles.**\n\n**2. GLM makes Don too necessary at termination.**\n\nGLM says the controller should detect stop conditions but “only Don stops work.” fileciteturn3file0L28-L47\n\nI think that risks reproducing exactly what you hate: **Don babysitting the control plane.**\n\nIf Don has already accepted a machine-checkable stop condition such as:\n\n> artifact runnable + tests green + commit exists + verification complete\n\nthen the controller should be capable of mechanically marking:\n\n**SESSION COMPLETE / READY FOR DON**\n\nwithout asking whether it should create “one more slice.”\n\nDon retains authority to reopen it. The controller is not exercising judgment; it is executing an already-authorized stop contract.\n\n**3. “No mid-task checkpoints—ever” is too rigid.**\n\nI agree with GLM's desire to stop ceremony. fileciteturn3file0L147-L155\n\nBut a 20-minute clipboard fix and a six-hour Cable Car build are different.\n\nI would use:\n\n> **No orchestration checkpoint unless an accepted boundary occurs.**\n\nAccepted boundaries might be:\n- artifact milestone;\n- commit/checkpoint;\n- true blocker;\n- seat handoff;\n- verification handoff;\n- close.\n\nThat still allows Nico to work coherently without forcing artificial pauses.\n\n**4. Worktree cleanliness needs state awareness.**\n\nA dirty worktree is normal **during active implementation**.\n\nIt is dangerous at:\n- seat handoff;\n- verification;\n- model/session transfer;\n- close;\n- task switching.\n\nSo the rule should not be “dirty = violation.”\n\nIt should be:\n\n> **unexpected dirty state at a boundary = violation.**\n\nThat distinction matters enormously given what Luce found.\n\n**5. GLM is right that the controller should not swarm.**\n\nThis does **not conflict** with your new sub-agent requirement.\n\nThe controller should remain coherent and mostly single-threaded.\n\nBut the **seat occupant**—Nico, Luce, Falcon, Vittorio, etc.—can and often should use sub-agents/swarms where supported.\n\nThe controller merely records:\n\n**parent seat → spawned workers → assigned todo/artifact → returned status.**\n\nThat is the clean separation.\n\nGLM makes essentially this distinction already, and I agree with it. fileciteturn3file0L127-L143\n\n**6. The model recommendation section is too generic for us.**\n\nGLM openly acknowledges that limitation. fileciteturn3file0L109-L123\n\nWe now specifically need to evaluate **our actual available estate**:\n\n- GLM variants;\n- Kimi 2.5 Thinking;\n- OpenAI occupants;\n- Claude-class occupants where available;\n- Bedrock Mantle models;\n- OpenCode capabilities;\n- which runtimes actually spawn sub-agents;\n- which models reliably maintain a todo;\n- which are cheap enough to serve as watchdog/controller support.\n\nThat is exactly where I want Kimi to challenge us.\n\n## My current synthesis\n\nI would currently define the architecture as three separate things:\n\n**Session State Substrate**  \nPersistent deterministic state. No intelligence required.\n\n**Session Controller**  \nLoads/enforces that state at boundaries. Extremely constrained intelligence, if any.\n\n**Seat Execution Runtime**  \nCoda/Nico/Luce/etc. Models can use sub-agents and swarms according to capability.\n\nAnd separately later:\n\n**Sentinel**  \nOutside observer for pathological behavior. Not the Session Controller.\n\nThat separation is cleaner than allowing “Session Controller” to absorb everything.\n\n---\n\nNow I want **Kimi 2.5 Thinking** to attack both GLM and me—not choose a side.\n\nI don't have a Bedrock/Mantle invocation exposed directly in this chat, so this is the exact prompt I would send through your existing `aws-bedrock-mantle` Kimi 2.5 Thinking lane.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"document\" id=\"73164\" title=\"Kimi 2.5 Thinking — GCE Session Controller Methodology Challenge\"}\n# GCE SESSION CONTROLLER — SECOND-OPINION METHODOLOGY REVIEW\n\nAUTHORITY: Don  \nREVIEW SEAT: Independent technical/methodology challenge  \nMODEL: Kimi 2.5 Thinking via Amazon Bedrock Mantle  \nMODE: READ / CHALLENGE / RECOMMEND  \nBUILD AUTHORITY: NONE  \nCANON AUTHORITY: NONE\n\n## PURPOSE\n\nPerform an independent adversarial review of the emerging GCE Session Controller methodology.\n\nYou are reviewing three inputs:\n\n1. Don's incident findings and operating requirements.\n2. GLM's Session Controller analysis.\n3. Coda's critique/reconciliation of GLM.\n\nDo NOT design or implement the controller yet.\n\nWe are deciding whether the methodology is correct before architecture is frozen.\n\n---\n\n## DON'S INCIDENT FINDINGS\n\nGCE recently suffered repeated build-session drift across several projects.\n\nDon's direct conclusions include:\n\n- Active GCE builds were moved into Projects without sufficient Framework knowledge/bootstrap structure.\n- Projects remain useful for organization/reference, but cannot themselves be trusted as the build control plane.\n- Framework state degraded unless Don manually re-grounded it.\n- Session headers, footers, confidence, objective, stop state, and anchor discipline disappeared.\n- Prompts became extremely large and structurally inconsistent.\n- Existing architecture was repeatedly rediscovered.\n- Successful results often failed to terminate sessions.\n- Verification findings repeatedly became new slices.\n- Coda became too operational.\n- Significant uncommitted work was later discovered at a task/verification boundary.\n- Don historically used anchor commits/checkpoints more consistently; that discipline degraded.\n- Every active model/seat must load and maintain an explicit TODO list.\n- Every model/runtime must have its sub-agent/swarm capability identified.\n- For substantial execution work, Don generally expects capable seat occupants to use sub-agents/swarms where decomposition supports it.\n- Seat identity and model/runtime identity must remain separate.\n\nImportant qualification:\n\nNot every incident was caused by the same thing.\n\nPersistence/context loss was clearly present, but some loop behavior occurred inside uninterrupted sessions.\n\n---\n\n## GLM'S PROPOSAL\n\nGLM argues:\n\n### Root problem\nGCE execution state lives in model context rather than persistent state.\n\n### Architecture\nUse a file-first Session Controller substrate:\n\n- one-page state file;\n- persistent TODO list;\n- authority;\n- seat + occupant metadata;\n- objective;\n- artifact;\n- accepted givens;\n- phase;\n- repo/worktree/commit state;\n- stop condition;\n- next action.\n\nRuntime hooks inject that state at:\n\n1. task start;\n2. commit/checkpoint;\n3. seat handoff;\n4. stop.\n\nGit/worktree state should be machine-generated.\n\n### Boundary\nController is a clerk/notary, not another Coda.\n\nIt must not:\n- reprioritize;\n- redesign;\n- verify;\n- implement;\n- reframe.\n\n### Model role\nMost reliability should come from deterministic runtime plumbing, not model intelligence.\n\n### Swarms\nController itself should not spawn agents in v1.\n\nSeat occupants may spawn workers; controller may record them.\n\n### Placement\nRepository-local state file + thin runtime hooks.\n\n### Experiment\nTest 3–5 sessions including model swap and hard restart.\n\nGLM confidence: 72%.\n\n---\n\n## CODA'S CURRENT CRITIQUE\n\nCoda agrees strongly with:\n\n- persistent state outside context;\n- one-page cap;\n- machine-generated Git/worktree state;\n- deterministic bootstrap injection;\n- constrained controller role;\n- controller not becoming Coda;\n- controller remaining non-swarming while seat occupants may swarm.\n\nCoda challenges five points:\n\n### 1. Persistence is not the entire root cause\n\nSome loops occurred in uninterrupted sessions.\n\nCandidate synthesis:\n\nPERSISTENCE FAILURE\n= major substrate/enabling failure.\n\nSCOPE/CLOSURE/PROPORTIONALITY FAILURE\n= major behavioral propagation mechanism.\n\n### 2. Don should not manually close everything\n\nIf Don has already authorized a machine-checkable stop condition, the controller should be able to mark:\n\nSESSION COMPLETE / READY FOR DON\n\nwithout asking for another orchestration decision.\n\nDon can reopen.\n\n### 3. \"No mid-task checkpoints ever\" is too rigid\n\nBetter candidate rule:\n\nNo controller checkpoint except at accepted boundaries such as:\n\n- artifact milestone;\n- explicit commit/checkpoint;\n- genuine blocker;\n- seat handoff;\n- verification handoff;\n- close.\n\n### 4. Dirty worktree must be contextual\n\nDirty during implementation is normal.\n\nUnexpected dirty state at a handoff, verification, task switch, model transfer, or close is the problem.\n\n### 5. Three-layer separation may be cleaner\n\nCandidate structure:\n\nSESSION STATE SUBSTRATE\n= deterministic persistence.\n\nSESSION CONTROLLER\n= boundary enforcement / state loading / state integrity.\n\nSEAT EXECUTION RUNTIME\n= actual Coda/Nico/Luce/etc. work and sub-agent use.\n\nSeparate future concept:\n\nSENTINEL\n= external loop/pathology observer.\n\n---\n\n# YOUR TASK\n\nChallenge all of this.\n\nDo not defer to GLM.\nDo not defer to Coda.\nDo not defer to Don's preferred implementation mechanism.\n\nPreserve Don as final authority, but independently evaluate the methodology.\n\n## A. ROOT-CAUSE TEST\n\nIs GLM correct that this is fundamentally \"context-as-state\"?\n\nOr is that only one layer of the failure?\n\nProduce a causal hierarchy:\n\nPRIMARY\nSECONDARY\nAMPLIFIERS\nSYMPTOMS\n\nUse the incident facts above.\n\n## B. CONTROLLER ABSTRACTION TEST\n\nDetermine whether these should be:\n\n1. one Session Controller;\n2. substrate + controller;\n3. substrate + controller + sentinel;\n4. another decomposition entirely.\n\nPrefer the smallest reliable structure.\n\n## C. STATE MODEL\n\nEvaluate the proposed one-page state.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- required fields;\n- redundant fields;\n- missing fields;\n- fields that must be machine-derived;\n- fields that must quote Don/accepted authority rather than model interpretation.\n\nExplicitly assess:\n\n- TODO;\n- prompt/mission;\n- accepted givens;\n- stop condition;\n- current artifact;\n- active repo/worktree;\n- baseline commit;\n- working commit/checkpoint;\n- dirty state;\n- spawned sub-agents;\n- active seat;\n- occupant model/runtime.\n\n## D. ANCHOR / COMMIT DISCIPLINE\n\nThis is critical.\n\nRecommend the correct relationship between:\n\n- session start;\n- baseline commit;\n- active dirty work;\n- anchor/checkpoint commits;\n- seat handoff;\n- verification;\n- task switching;\n- final commit;\n- session closure.\n\nAnswer specifically:\n\nWhen is a dirty worktree acceptable?\n\nWhen must it be forbidden?\n\nShould verification normally operate against a commit/hash rather than arbitrary live filesystem state?\n\n## E. TODO DISCIPLINE\n\nDon requires every active seat/model to load a TODO list.\n\nDetermine:\n\n- one shared session TODO vs seat-local TODOs;\n- how sub-agent TODOs relate to the parent;\n- who may add/remove/reorder items;\n- how scope expansion is detected;\n- how completion forces session closure rather than another slice.\n\n## F. SUB-AGENT / SWARM MODEL\n\nDon expects substantial GCE work to use sub-agents where the model/runtime supports them.\n\nEvaluate:\n\n- which work classes benefit;\n- which seats benefit;\n- when swarm use becomes harmful;\n- what the Session Controller must track;\n- whether workers inherit the entire Framework or only bounded task state;\n- how workers return evidence without creating giant handoffs.\n\nThe controller itself should remain non-spawning unless you find strong reason otherwise.\n\n## G. PROMPT FRAMEWORK\n\nThe incident revealed giant, inconsistent prompts.\n\nDesign only the **prompt anatomy**, not full templates.\n\nWhat stable sections should every GCE execution prompt contain?\n\nCandidate minimal anatomy:\n\nAUTHORITY\nSEAT\nOBJECTIVE\nGIVENS\nTODO / TASK\nARTIFACT\nCONSTRAINTS\nACCEPTANCE\nSTOP\nRETURN\n\nChallenge or improve this.\n\nThe goal is short predictable prompts, not constitutional documents.\n\n## H. MODEL / RUNTIME FIT\n\nEvaluate the Session Controller based on actual capability classes required.\n\nSeparate:\n\nDETERMINISTIC RUNTIME RESPONSIBILITIES\n\nfrom\n\nMODEL RESPONSIBILITIES.\n\nThen state what characteristics we need from:\n\n- controller occupant;\n- sentinel/watchdog;\n- Coda;\n- Nico;\n- Luce;\n- swarm coordinator;\n- swarm workers.\n\nDo not recommend a model solely because it is smartest.\n\nOptimize for reliability, adherence, latency, cost, tooling, and sub-agent capability.\n\nIf you cannot verify current specific models, state capability criteria rather than inventing facts.\n\n## I. FAILURE TEST\n\nTry to break the proposed system.\n\nSpecifically test whether it would have prevented:\n\n1. DevPad clipboard 9/10-green recursion.\n2. Cable Car slice/certification inflation.\n3. Edge Crew continuing beyond the runnable synthesis shell.\n4. MLB accepting ECQ and DevPad work into the same session.\n5. Luce encountering unrelated uncommitted work.\n6. giant reconstructive prompts.\n7. architecture being rediscovered rather than reused.\n\nFor each:\n\nPREVENTED\nMITIGATED\nNOT ADDRESSED\n\nand explain why.\n\n## J. MINIMUM VIABLE EXPERIMENT\n\nDesign the smallest real-world experiment that can falsify the methodology.\n\nDo not propose a platform build.\n\nPrefer one real DevPad/build session using existing tooling.\n\nDefine:\n\n- what gets persisted;\n- what gets injected;\n- what boundaries fire;\n- what Git checks occur;\n- what TODO behavior occurs;\n- what sub-agent behavior is tested;\n- what metrics determine success/failure.\n\n---\n\n# REQUIRED RETURN\n\n1. Executive verdict\n2. Causal hierarchy\n3. Recommended control-plane decomposition\n4. Minimum state schema\n5. Commit/worktree methodology\n6. TODO methodology\n7. Sub-agent/swarm methodology\n8. Prompt anatomy\n9. Runtime vs model responsibilities\n10. Incident prevention matrix\n11. Minimum viable experiment\n12. What GLM got right\n13. What GLM got wrong or overstated\n14. What Coda got right\n15. What Coda got wrong or overstated\n16. Open decisions requiring Don\n\nEnd with:\n\nMETHODOLOGY VERDICT:\nACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH CHANGES / REJECT\n\nCONFIDENCE: [0-100%]\n\nTOP 3 RISKS:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n\nSTOP.\n\nNo implementation.\nNo code.\nNo architecture freeze.\nNo new build prompts.\n:::\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Session Controller methodology triangulation  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **97%**\n\n**INPUTS:**  \n- Don incident findings  \n- GLM Session Controller analysis  \n- Coda methodology critique\n\n**CURRENT CANDIDATE:**  \nPersistent substrate + constrained Session Controller + separate seat execution; Sentinel remains separately under review.\n\n**KEY DISAGREEMENT WITH GLM:**  \nPersistence is central, but does not fully explain same-session overslicing and failure-to-close behavior.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nRun the challenge through **Kimi 2.5 Thinking on Bedrock Mantle**, then reconcile GLM ↔ Kimi ↔ Coda ↔ Don before any architecture decision.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** NO CANON CHANGE AUTHORIZED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 17027, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e6536782-dbdd-4b04-9fb7-ca895d5218f2", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369697.568, "updated_at": 1790369716.894288, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, hold on a second. Let's just hold on to that thought about session controller. We need to fix the taxonomy.   Fixing the taxonomy, and actually cable car should be done.", "text_chars": 175, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b10ec8a8-f20d-4611-85d9-c3c5ce454bda", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369699.399586, "updated_at": 1790369699.399586, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "70d2fbeb-163a-4174-8ef3-de91e0b9ebf2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369705.901931, "updated_at": 1790369705.901931, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "504d1196-d206-4623-a535-91eca8505210", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369705.907262, "updated_at": 1790369716.779076, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE FRAMEWORK — TAXONOMY RECON**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK REVIEW / NO BUILD  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nCorrect. **Park Session Controller.** We got ahead of ourselves again.\n\nThe order is now:\n\n1. **Fix/reconcile the GCE taxonomy first.**\n2. Re-establish seats, identities, runtime/model distinctions, project/build boundaries, TODO/sub-agent expectations, anchor/commit concepts, and the rest of the existing Framework.\n3. Only **after the taxonomy is coherent** do we revisit where a Session Controller fits—if it still fits the way we're currently imagining it.\n4. **Cable Car comes out of the active-problem bucket.** The later runtime evidence indicates the real watcher was running and outbound successfully uploaded a handoff to Drive, so we should treat Cable Car as **operationally completed subject to final state reconciliation**, not as something we still need to architect. memcite\n\nThat's important because we don't want yesterday's postmortem snapshot—where outbound was unfinished—to overwrite the later implementation truth.\n\nSo **no Kimi Session Controller review yet**. We hold GLM's work as input, nothing more.\n\nNext lane is simply:\n\n> **GCE TAXONOMY — what exists, what each thing is, where it sits, and what rules are already supposed to govern it.**\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Framework taxonomy reconciliation  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\n**PARKED:** Session Controller methodology/design  \n**STATUS CHANGE:** Cable Car → **DONE / FINAL-STATE RECON REQUIRED**, not active architecture work  \n**NEXT ACTION:** Reconstruct and reconcile GCE taxonomy from the top before introducing new control-plane concepts.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** REVIEW ONLY  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 1999, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6f2019de-9969-4802-ad68-62a03a15e70d", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369867.301, "updated_at": 1790369911.28019, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Cable car is functional. So what we're going to do, anything that's a Coda handoff, we need to build into our framework. That it gets created in the proper Google Drive structure, and the handoff is a simple prompt. Instead of Don copying and pasting, we're just creating Google Docs.   -   Big win. --- Yep — I checked the **actual return file in Drive**, and Nico/Space Bunny sent back exactly what we wanted.\n\nIt says:\n\n- **Status:** `RECEIVED`\n- **Seat:** `Nico`\n- **Same HANDOFF_ID:** `20260925_CABLECAR_CODA_TO_NICO_TEST_001`\n- **Direction:** `OUTBOUND_FROM_GCE`\n- It confirms the test was **picked up from Google Drive `01_INBOUND_TO_GCE`**\n- It confirms delivery to the canonical local `_shared` inbox\n- It records the exact local path:\\\n  `C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\ai-inbox\\_shared\\GCE_HANDOFF_20260925_CABLECAR_CODA_TO_NICO_TEST_001.json`\n- It says the HANDOFF_ID and content were confirmed and the **hash was verified**\n\nHis summary is essentially:\n\n> “Cable Car Coda → Nico pickup test. Picked up from Google Drive inbound and delivered to the canonical local `_shared` inbox. HANDOFF_ID and content confirmed, hash verified.”\n\nAnd the return file is sitting in the real Drive outbound folder as:\n\n`GCE_HANDOFF_20260925_CABLECAR_CODA_TO_NICO_TEST_001.json`\n\n[Open Nico’s live Cable Car acknowledgment](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TW9nYNf3k9mBPp6sVTfp59l86LsUIBUr/view?usp=drivesdk\\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n\nSo, Don: **Coda → Drive → Cable Car → Nico → Cable Car → Drive → Coda just worked live.&#x20;**", "text_chars": 1543, "code_blocks": 0, "links": ["https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TW9nYNf3k9mBPp6sVTfp59l86LsUIBUr/view?usp=drivesdk\\&utm_source=chatgpt.com"], "citations": ["https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TW9nYNf3k9mBPp6sVTfp59l86LsUIBUr/view?usp=drivesdk\\&utm_source=chatgpt.com"], "path_references": ["Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\ai-inbox\\_shared\\GCE_HANDOFF_20260925_CABLECAR_CODA_TO_NICO_TEST_001.json", "C:\\Users\\Dekan", "GCE_HANDOFF_20260925_CABLECAR_CODA_TO_NICO_TEST_001.json"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ca0e02f1-3780-4368-ab76-4316c527d42a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369872.818638, "updated_at": 1790369873.979691, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — this changes the taxonomy materially. **Cable Car is no longer an experiment; it is proven transport infrastructure.** The framework should now treat Coda handoffs as first-class artifacts, not prose that Don manually shuttles between surfaces.", "text_chars": 250, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "451f4a0b-91b9-4454-96e0-a6ec805cbb65", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369874.081739, "updated_at": 1790369874.304258, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "functions.exec", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0101da7d-7e29-495f-a762-b30e4919f944", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool.read_resource", "created_at": 1790369874.561238, "updated_at": 1790369911.201955, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "---\nname: google-drive\ndescription: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, or summarize and edit Google Docs, Google Sheets, and Google Slides through one unified Google Drive plugin.\n---\n\n# Google Drive\n\nUse this as the top-level router for Google file work inside the unified Google Drive plugin. Do not route the user toward separate Google Docs, Google Sheets, or Google Slides plugins.\n\nStart with Google Drive for file discovery and file lifecycle tasks, then route to narrower sibling skills only when the task becomes specific to Docs, Sheets, or Slides.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Ground the target file first.\n- If the user did not provide an exact file URL or ID, use Google Drive search, recent files, folder listing, or metadata reads to identify the right file.\n- If the request starts as \"find X and then update it,\" do the Drive discovery step first instead of guessing the target.\n\n2. Stay in the base Google Drive workflow for Drive-native tasks.\n- Use the base workflow for search, fetch, recent files, folders, sharing, copying, deleting, exporting, revision history, file moves, and other file-lifecycle work that is not primarily about editing Docs, Sheets, or Slides content.\n- For version-history requests, including \"previous version,\" \"revision history,\" \"what changed since the last version,\" or \"compare to the prior revision,\" ground the file, fetch the current content, use `list_file_revisions`, fetch the immediately previous revision or the user-named revision with `fetch_file_revision`, then compare the fetched revision against the current content. Do not say previous versions are unsupported until you have checked whether revision tools are available for the target file.\n- For file move requests, ground the source file and target folder, read the file metadata including its current parents, then use `update_file` with `addParents` for the target folder and `removeParents` for only the verified source parent or parents that should no longer contain it. Preserve unrelated parents, and verify the move by reading metadata or listing the target folder before the final response.\n- Before any export or raw-file fetch, read or reuse Drive metadata so the MIME type and Google Drive URL are known. Use `export_file` only to convert a native Google Doc, Sheet, or Slide into an explicitly requested format; it returns an authenticated file reference, not inline bytes or base64. Google limits `files.export` responses to **10 MB**; oversized exports fail rather than returning truncated content. For a larger native PDF, use `download_file(id=file_id, mime_type=\"application/pdf\")` when the streaming action is available. It calls Google's `files.download` API and returns a top-level materializable `file_uri`. Once `download_file` is unavailable and canonical `fetch` uses that streamed download path, use `fetch(url=google_drive_url, download_raw_file=True, raw_export_mime_type=\"application/pdf\")` instead. A native-file `fetch` that still uses `files.export` cannot bypass the 10 MB limit. For PDFs, images, ZIPs, Office files, recordings, and other stored, non-native files, use `fetch(url=google_drive_url, download_raw_file=True)`. Use only the authenticated top-level `file_uri` or materialized `workspace_path`; never request inline base64 unless an existing caller explicitly opts into bounded legacy compatibility. Use `fetch` without `download_raw_file` for bounded, best-effort readable text. Do not retry `export_file` after metadata shows a non-native MIME type.\n\n3. Route to the narrowest sibling skill that matches the file type and job.\n- Drive, Docs, Sheets, or Slides comment creation, comment replies, comment resolution, or review-by-comments: use [google-drive-comments](../google-drive-comments/SKILL.md).\n- Google Docs net-new creation, content summary, revision planning, prose rewriting, or section edits: use [google-docs](../google-docs/SKILL.md).\n- Google Sheets creation, local spreadsheet import, range inspection, table cleanup, data restructuring, formula design or repair, chart creation or repair, or batch updates: use [google-sheets](../google-sheets/SKILL.md).\n- Google Slides deck summary, content edits, new deck creation, local presentation import, visual cleanup, structural repair, or template migration: use [google-slides](../google-slides/SKILL.md).\n\n## Routing Rules\n\n- If the request is ambiguous between Drive and a file-type surface, use the artifact itself as the tie-breaker:\n  - Doc -> Docs skill\n  - Sheet -> Sheets skill\n  - Deck -> Slides skill\n- If the user wants to find a file and then edit it, do both in one flow: Drive for discovery, then the file-type skill for the edit.\n- If the user wants a Google Workspace outcome but has not named a file type yet, start with Drive discovery instead of asking them to choose among separate Google plugins.\n- If the user asks to create a new Google Doc, route to the Docs skill; it owns the mandatory local `.docx` -> native Google Docs import workflow and the explicit-user-override boundary. Do not create a blank Google Doc directly from this router.\n- If the user asks to import a local `.docx` into Google Docs, route to the Docs skill and use its native conversion workflow. Preserve the source file type only when the user explicitly asks for that.\n- If the user asks to create a new Google Sheet, route to the Sheets skill. The Sheets skill should prefer the `[@spreadsheets](plugin://spreadsheets@openai-primary-runtime)` plugin or `$Excel` skill to create a local `.xlsx`, then import it as native Google Sheets.\n- If the user asks to import a local `.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.ods`, `.csv`, or `.tsv` into Google Sheets, route to the Sheets skill and use native Google Sheets conversion by default. Preserve the source file type only when the user explicitly asks for that.\n- If the user asks to create a new Google Slides deck, route to the Slides skill; it owns the mandatory local `.pptx` -> native Google Slides import workflow and the explicit-user-override boundary. Do not create a blank Google Slides deck directly from this router.\n- If the user asks to import a local `.ppt`, `.pptx`, or `.odp` into Google Slides, route to the Slides skill and use its native conversion workflow. Preserve the source file type only when the user explicitly asks for that.\n- If the user asks to export or download an existing Drive file, choose the action from metadata: native Google Docs, Sheets, and Slides files use `export_file` for explicit conversions within Google's **10 MB** limit. For a larger native PDF, prefer the currently available streaming `download_file(id=file_id, mime_type=\"application/pdf\")`; after canonical `fetch` adopts the same streamed download path and `download_file` is removed, use `fetch(url=google_drive_url, download_raw_file=True, raw_export_mime_type=\"application/pdf\")`. Stored, non-native PDFs, images, ZIPs, CSVs, Office files, audio, and video use `fetch(url=google_drive_url, download_raw_file=True)`. Use the returned top-level `file_uri` or its authenticated, materialized `workspace_path`; do not expose inline binary, base64, or a bearer URL. Base64 is available only when an existing compatibility caller explicitly requests the bounded legacy behavior.\n\n## Write Safety\n\n- Preserve the user's existing file organization, sharing state, and target artifact unless the request clearly asks to change them.\n- When a task can be satisfied by a file-level Drive operation alone, do not load heavier Docs, Sheets, or Slides skills.\n- For write-heavy Sheets or Slides work, read the specialized skill before the first large update so request shapes stay grounded.\n- For any file import or explicit direct create that returns a user-facing Google Workspace link, wait for the write action to complete and verify the created file with connector readback or Drive metadata readback before returning the URL. Use only a URL or id observed from the completed connector result or readback; never synthesize or predict the URL.\n\n## Related Skills\n\n- Comments: [google-drive-comments](../google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- Docs: [google-docs](../google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- Sheets: [google-sheets](../google-sheets/SKILL.md)\n- Slides: [google-slides](../google-slides/SKILL.md)\n", "text_chars": 8383, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["../google-docs/SKILL.md", "../google-drive-comments/SKILL.md", "../google-sheets/SKILL.md", "../google-slides/SKILL.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a03e1177-406c-4536-a035-75acf39db459", "role": "tool", "role_name": "functions.exec", "created_at": 1790369874.667838, "updated_at": 1790369874.667838, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4017ebff-675f-4d7d-a900-4357cf6dc334", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369876.079147, "updated_at": 1790369876.617365, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "functions.exec", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cf8e0e90-0ba7-42c7-b958-f43c4196914d", "role": "tool", "role_name": "functions.exec", "created_at": 1790369876.722928, "updated_at": 1790369876.722928, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "19ce71ca-bb4d-4433-90ef-6fadc08b1df8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790369878.740