ifact, not the durable project/canonical artifact.**\n\nThe earlier design already made that distinction explicitly: **transport archive != project canon**. fileciteturn7file1L1-L1\n\nSo this should become a bootstrap-level handoff lifecycle rule, probably something as compact as:\n\n**RECEIVE → INGEST → OPERATE/RETURN → MARK COMPLETE → ARCHIVE TRANSPORT ARTIFACT**\n\nNo cleanup crew should be needed for normal handoffs. Cleanup crew is for exceptions, stale residue, conflicts, broken lifecycle state, or historical junk.\n\nThat also means the seat receiving the handoff owns the **normal lifecycle completion**, while Cable Car/Pigeon handles transport mechanics.\n\n## What I would mark right now\n\n**VERIFIED:** archive behavior exists in Cable Car methodology/design.  \n**NOT VERIFIED:** universal bootstrap enforcement across Coda/Nico/Luce/etc.  \n**GAP:** lifecycle ownership is not yet proven as a seat-level bootstrap invariant.  \n**RECOMMENDED TAXONOMY CHANGE:** make handoff lifecycle completion a universal bootstrap responsibility.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Framework taxonomy — handoff lifecycle  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **98%**\n\n**FINDING:**  \nAutomatic archive was designed for Cable Car, but I cannot verify it is universally baked into the active bootstrap.\n\n**NEW NON-NEGOTIABLE:**  \nAny seat/model that receives and ingests a handoff must complete its transport lifecycle and archive the completed handoff automatically.\n\n**EXCEPTION:**  \nDo not archive/remove canonical project or Framework records merely because their transport copy is complete.\n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nWhen we review the bootstrap taxonomy, explicitly locate and normalize the handoff lifecycle rule across all seats.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** REVIEW ONLY  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 3555, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d145058d-381a-4b55-b9a4-6f0f64b1a08b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370013.863, "updated_at": 1790370059.757528, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Hey, cool. We'll hold that thought. Hold that thought as our running checklist to do once we get back. Thing with Space Bunny, and I think it's the internet, the packet loss is killing Don. Can you give— oh, my room is ready. Can you give Don I need, I need Luce, actual Big Pickle, to test the packet loss and everything. Or give Don the prompt. You could just give it to Don in PowerShell. Do it all in one. I'm not doing 5 different fucking PowerShell scripts.", "text_chars": 463, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fd9c9ed0-033e-438f-9816-eaa72c35c4b7", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370018.163137, "updated_at": 1790370018.163137, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8d1fd91f-033c-4f5f-b796-5202c6653905", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370018.166715, "updated_at": 1790370018.166715, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f90f0ffa-5290-4d0e-98b4-4e09013f8418", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370024.402894, "updated_at": 1790370026.851654, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2d967883-96c6-42be-90c5-49058387186d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370026.869641, "updated_at": 1790370026.869641, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5ca70be0-6ab9-43a2-a4af-8eb8e64a8928", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370026.874577, "updated_at": 1790370059.013895, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE — LUCE NETWORK TRUTH TEST**  \n**SEAT:** Luce | **OCCUPANT:** Big Pickle  \n**MODE:** VERIFY / EVIDENCE ONLY  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYep. **One PowerShell block. One run.** No five-script bullshit.\n\nGive this whole thing to **Luce / actual Big Pickle** and have it run from the machine Space Bunny is using. It collects Wi-Fi state, gateway loss, Internet loss, DNS, latency/jitter, route evidence, and a `pathping` trace into one report.\n\n```powershell\n# ================================================================\n# GCE / LUCE — NETWORK + PACKET LOSS TRUTH TEST\n# MODE: READ-ONLY DIAGNOSTIC\n# PURPOSE: Determine whether Space Bunny instability is caused by\n#         local Wi-Fi, gateway loss, ISP/upstream loss, DNS, or WAN.\n# ================================================================\n\n$ErrorActionPreference = \"Continue\"\n$ts = Get-Date -Format \"yyyyMMdd_HHmmss\"\n$out = \"$env:USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\GCE_LUCE_NETWORK_TEST_$ts.txt\"\n\nfunction Log($s = \"\") {\n    $s | Tee-Object -FilePath $out -Append\n}\n\nfunction Ping-Test {\n    param(\n        [string]$Name,\n        [string]$Target,\n        [int]$Count = 100\n    )\n\n    Log \"\"\n    Log \"============================================================\"\n    Log \"PING TEST: $Name [$Target] — $Count packets\"\n    Log \"============================================================\"\n\n    $r = Test-Connection -TargetName $Target -Count $Count -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n\n    if (-not $r) {\n        Log \"RESULT: NO RESPONSES\"\n        return\n    }\n\n    $times = @($r | ForEach-Object { $_.Latency })\n    $received = $times.Count\n    $lost = $Count - $received\n    $lossPct = [math]::Round(($lost / $Count) * 100, 2)\n\n    $avg = [math]::Round(($times | Measure-Object -Average).Average, 2)\n    $min = ($times | Measure-Object -Minimum).Minimum\n    $max = ($times | Measure-Object -Maximum).Maximum\n\n    $jitterSamples = @()\n    for ($i = 1; $i -lt $times.Count; $i++) {\n        $jitterSamples += [math]::Abs($times[$i] - $times[$i-1])\n    }\n\n    $jitter = if ($jitterSamples.Count) {\n        [math]::Round(($jitterSamples | Measure-Object -Average).Average, 2)\n    } else { 0 }\n\n    Log \"Sent:       $Count\"\n    Log \"Received:   $received\"\n    Log \"Lost:       $lost\"\n    Log \"LOSS:       $lossPct %\"\n    Log \"Latency:    min=$min ms / avg=$avg ms / max=$max ms\"\n    Log \"Jitter avg: $jitter ms\"\n\n    if ($lossPct -gt 2) {\n        Log \"LUCE FLAG: SEVERE PACKET LOSS\"\n    }\n    elseif ($lossPct -gt 0) {\n        Log \"LUCE FLAG: PACKET LOSS PRESENT\"\n    }\n    elseif ($jitter -gt 30) {\n        Log \"LUCE FLAG: HIGH JITTER\"\n    }\n    else {\n        Log \"LUCE FLAG: CLEAN\"\n    }\n}\n\n\"============================================================\" | Set-Content $out\nLog \"GCE LUCE / BIG PICKLE — NETWORK TRUTH TEST\"\nLog \"Timestamp: $(Get-Date)\"\nLog \"Computer:  $env:COMPUTERNAME\"\nLog \"User:      $env:USERNAME\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"### NETWORK ADAPTERS\"\nGet-NetAdapter |\n    Sort-Object Status,Name |\n    Format-Table Name,InterfaceDescription,Status,LinkSpeed -Auto |\n    Out-String | Log\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"### WIFI RADIO / SIGNAL\"\n(netsh wlan show interfaces | Out-String) | Log\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"### IP CONFIGURATION\"\n(ipconfig /all | Out-String) | Log\n\n# Determine active IPv4 default gateway\n$gw = (Get-NetRoute -DestinationPrefix \"0.0.0.0/0\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |\n    Sort-Object RouteMetric |\n    Select-Object -First 1).NextHop\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"ACTIVE DEFAULT GATEWAY: $gw\"\n\nif ($gw) {\n    Ping-Test \"LOCAL GATEWAY\" $gw 100\n}\n\nPing-Test \"CLOUDFLARE\" \"1.1.1.1\" 100\nPing-Test \"GOOGLE DNS\" \"8.8.8.8\" 100\nPing-Test \"DNS/HOSTNAME TEST\" \"cloudflare.com\" 100\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"HTTPS CONNECTIVITY\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\n\nforeach ($hostName in @(\"1.1.1.1\",\"google.com\",\"github.com\")) {\n    Log \"\"\n    Log \"--- $hostName :443 ---\"\n    (Test-NetConnection $hostName -Port 443 -InformationLevel Detailed |\n        Format-List | Out-String) | Log\n}\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"DNS TEST\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\n\nforeach ($name in @(\"google.com\",\"github.com\",\"openai.com\")) {\n    Log \"\"\n    Log \"--- $name ---\"\n    try {\n        (Resolve-DnsName $name -ErrorAction Stop |\n            Format-Table -Auto | Out-String) | Log\n    }\n    catch {\n        Log \"DNS FAILURE: $($_.Exception.Message)\"\n    }\n}\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"ROUTE TRACE — 1.1.1.1\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\n(tracert -d 1.1.1.1 | Out-String) | Log\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"PATHPING — 1.1.1.1\"\nLog \"THIS IS THE LONG TEST; LET IT FINISH.\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\n(pathping -n 1.1.1.1 | Out-String) | Log\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"LUCE INTERPRETATION RULES\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"1. Gateway loss > 0% = LOCAL WIFI / AP / HOTEL NETWORK problem.\"\nLog \"2. Gateway clean but 1.1.1.1 + 8.8.8.8 loss = ISP / hotel upstream problem.\"\nLog \"3. IP targets clean but hostname/DNS bad = DNS problem.\"\nLog \"4. 0% loss but large jitter/spikes = unstable/congested network.\"\nLog \"5. PATHPING should identify where persistent loss begins.\"\nLog \"6. Do NOT treat loss on one intermediate router alone as proof; routers may deprioritize ICMP.\"\nLog \"7. Final destination loss + application disconnects are the important correlation.\"\n\nLog \"\"\nLog \"============================================================\"\nLog \"REPORT COMPLETE\"\nLog \"Saved to:\"\nLog $out\nLog \"============================================================\"\n\nWrite-Host \"\"\nWrite-Host \"LUCE NETWORK TEST COMPLETE\"\nWrite-Host \"REPORT: $out\"\nWrite-Host \"\"\nnotepad $out\n```\n\n### What I want Luce to determine\n\n**Do not fix anything yet.** Luce should return only:\n\n> **LOCAL / HOTEL-WIFI / UPSTREAM-ISP / DNS / CLEAN-BUT-JITTERY / INCONCLUSIVE**  \n> Gateway packet loss: X%  \n> Internet packet loss: X%  \n> Average latency: X ms  \n> Jitter: X ms  \n> Worst hop: X  \n> **Does the evidence reasonably explain Space Bunny disconnecting/stalling? YES / NO / PARTIAL**\n\nIf that gateway test starts dropping packets, that's especially useful because it tells us the problem is happening **before we're even reaching the Internet**.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**SESSION FOOTER**\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Temporary Space Bunny connectivity diagnosis  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n**RUNNING TAXONOMY CHECKLIST:** HELD — resume after network issue  \n**NEXT ACTION:** One Luce/Big Pickle network truth run; inspect one report.  \n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 7063, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["ts.txt"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4d2fe694-b3f5-4c54-b4f1-74489c8abd28", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370101.075, "updated_at": 1790370161.147692, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, now your turn while I'm investigating this. Your turn is now to go through and reconcile our discussion. Do an anchor commit because we've updated the session footer and header on your artifacts to get ready to implement. Once I move rooms, we're going to go through and start framework conversation because I'm— I think I'm out of 2 black holes already.", "text_chars": 360, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "509a02fd-efc9-4bdf-8c82-58c8058ac887", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370110.601731, "updated_at": 1790370160.347297, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "75004e32-3f6d-404b-8f27-de2156783251", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370160.347583, "updated_at": 1790370160.34834, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I’m reconciling only what we actually settled in this thread and freezing it as the **current session anchor**—no new doctrine, no Session Controller design, no build work. The network/Luce detour stays outside the Framework taxonomy state.", "text_chars": 240, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7b87e640-51bc-4077-9b84-34fb7dfccf9f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370160.347902, "updated_at": 1790370160.347902, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "688c7411-75ee-4363-a7b1-8f0707bcfc6c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370160.348599, "updated_at": 1790370160.964836, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE FRAMEWORK — ANCHOR COMMIT  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK RECONCILIATION / PRE-IMPLEMENTATION  \n**DATE:** 2026-09-25  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n**BUILD AUTHORITY:** NONE  \n**CANON AUTHORITY:** REVIEW / NOT YET PROMOTED  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n## ANCHOR STATE\n\nThis is the current reconciled state from tonight’s discussion. I am treating this as the **session anchor commit** for our next Framework conversation.\n\n### 1. Projects\n\n**Projects are organizational containers, not trusted GCE build control planes by themselves.**\n\nGood uses:\n- reference material;\n- dumping grounds;\n- topical organization;\n- historical/project discussion;\n- bounded non-build work.\n\nActive GCE builds require Framework execution controls that survive the surrounding container.\n\nThe move into under-grounded Projects was a major enabling condition for the recent drift.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Framework persistence failure\n\nThe Framework was increasingly living in conversational context instead of remaining structurally present.\n\nObserved symptoms:\n\n- session headers disappearing;\n- footers disappearing;\n- confidence disappearing;\n- objectives becoming implicit;\n- prompts becoming huge;\n- prompt structure becoming inconsistent;\n- architecture being rediscovered;\n- successful work failing to close;\n- Coda becoming excessively operational;\n- seat/task boundaries weakening.\n\nThis failure occurred strongly enough that it reproduced **inside this postmortem conversation itself** until Don called it out.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Prompt structure\n\nCurrent prompts became too long and structurally inconsistent.\n\nFuture Framework review must define a stable **framework within the prompt frame**.\n\nCandidate elements already identified:\n\n**AUTHORITY → SEAT → OBJECTIVE → GIVENS → TODO/TASK → ARTIFACT → CONSTRAINTS → ACCEPTANCE → STOP → RETURN**\n\nExact taxonomy remains to be reviewed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. TODO lists — NON-NEGOTIABLE\n\nEvery active GCE seat/model occupant must load and maintain an explicit **active TODO list**.\n\nA seat must not wake up and reconstruct its work from chat history or a giant prompt.\n\nThis applies regardless of:\n- identity;\n- provider;\n- model;\n- runtime.\n\n**SEAT != MODEL remains intact.**\n\n---\n\n### 5. Sub-agents / swarms — NON-NEGOTIABLE CAPABILITY REVIEW\n\nEvery model/runtime used by GCE must have its sub-agent capability explicitly identified.\n\nWe need to know:\n\n- sub-agents: YES / NO / LIMITED;\n- parallel execution capability;\n- swarm capability;\n- context inheritance;\n- tool inheritance;\n- coordination behavior.\n\nDon’s expected default:\n\n> **Substantial work should generally exploit sub-agents/swarm execution when the model/runtime supports it and the work decomposes cleanly.**\n\nThis belongs to the **occupant/runtime capability profile**, not seat identity.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Session Controller\n\n**PARKED.**\n\nGLM produced useful analysis.\n\nCurrent useful hypothesis:\n\n- persistent state substrate;\n- constrained Session Controller;\n- separate seat execution runtime;\n- Sentinel potentially separate.\n\nBut:\n\n> **Taxonomy gets reconciled first.**\n\nNo architecture freeze or implementation until the existing Framework is understood again.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Cable Car\n\n**CABLE CAR IS FUNCTIONAL.**\n\nLive round trip has been proven:\n\n**Coda → Drive → Cable Car → Nico → Cable Car → Drive → Coda**\n\nSame `HANDOFF_ID` survived the round trip, delivery reached canonical `_shared`, and the return recorded successful receipt and hash verification. fileciteturn5file0L2-L21\n\nCable Car is therefore no longer an open architecture problem.\n\nIt belongs in Framework taxonomy as **transport infrastructure**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Coda handoffs\n\nCoda handoffs become **first-class Framework artifacts**.\n\nDesired Don UX:\n\n> “Send this to Nico.”\n\nNot:\n\n> copy prompt → change windows → paste terminal → collect return → manually shuttle everything back.\n\nExpected direction:\n\n**Coda intent → canonical Drive handoff artifact → Cable Car → target seat → return artifact → Coda**\n\nHuman-facing Google Docs are desired for simple readable handoffs.\n\nThe current proven transport mechanism uses the structured Cable Car envelope; exact Google Doc/envelope integration remains an implementation detail.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Handoff lifecycle / archive\n\nRunning checklist item:\n\nNormal handoffs should not accumulate forever.\n\nExisting Cable Car thinking already included:\n\n**READY → TRANSPORTED → ACKNOWLEDGED → COMPLETED → ARCHIVED**\n\nand explicitly contemplated automatic archive of completed transport artifacts. fileciteturn7file0L1-L1\n\nDon’s clarified requirement is stronger:\n\n> **Once a handoff has been received, ingested, acted upon, and returned/completed, the responsible system/seat should automatically move the transport artifact to archive.**\n\nThis includes Coda.\n\nCleanup crew handles:\n- broken lifecycle items;\n- stale residue;\n- conflicts;\n- historical cleanup.\n\nIt should **not** be needed for ordinary handoff housekeeping.\n\nImportant distinction:\n\n**transport archive != project/framework canon.**\n\nThis is on the taxonomy/bootstrap checklist; universal enforcement has not yet been verified.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Git / worktree discipline\n\nA significant concern remains:\n\nLuce encountered substantial uncommitted work during another task boundary.\n\nThat should not happen without the dirty state being intentional and attributable.\n\nThe Framework review must reconstruct:\n\n- baseline commits;\n- anchor commits;\n- checkpoint discipline;\n- worktree boundaries;\n- handoff commit expectations;\n- verification target expectations;\n- task-switching cleanliness.\n\nCurrent supported principle:\n\n> Dirty state may be normal during active implementation.  \n> Unexpected dirty state at handoff, verification, task switch, or closure is not.\n\nExact doctrine remains to be reconciled.\n\n---\n\n### 11. DevPad clipboard clarification\n\nDon clarified the actual original intent:\n\n> Copy a prompt from Coda/ChatGPT and paste it normally into a DevPad terminal session.\n\nThat should have been the central acceptance test.\n\nThe broader right-click clipboard exploration was secondary to that user journey.\n\nDon accepts partial responsibility for not defining this objective sharply enough at the beginning.\n\n---\n\n### 12. MLB / Edge Crew responsibility clarification\n\nDon accepts more responsibility for the Edge Crew experimental contamination because the chat was intentionally experimental and allowed adjacent work.\n\nDon also accepts that the MLB DevPad request assumed reuse of an already-existing DevPad pattern without explicitly grounding that assumption.\n\nThose concessions do **not** explain unrelated bounded chats abandoning their lanes.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Cross-session incident conclusion\n\nCurrent reconciled causal picture:\n\n**MAJOR ENABLING CONDITION**  \nUnder-grounded Project execution weakened Framework persistence.\n\n**PROPAGATION MECHANISM**  \nCoda compensated with oversized reconstructive prompts and excessive orchestration.\n\n**EXECUTION FAILURE**  \nSuccessful artifacts did not reliably terminate work.\n\n**AMPLIFIERS**  \n- overslicing;\n- recursive verification;\n- missing stop conditions;\n- inconsistent prompt anatomy;\n- architecture rediscovery;\n- weak commit/worktree boundaries.\n\n**NOT SUPPORTED AS PRIMARY CAUSE**  \nModel quality.\n\n---\n\n## WHAT IS NOT BEING CARRIED INTO THE NEXT SESSION\n\nDo **not** reopen as assumptions:\n\n- Cable Car architecture is unfinished.\n- Session Controller architecture has already been accepted.\n- Projects themselves provide build discipline.\n- Coda should manually generate huge standalone handoff prompts.\n- Don should be the manual transport bus.\n- Luce caused the recursive verification loops.\n- every dirty worktree is automatically bad.\n- every model should swarm merely because it can.\n\n---\n\n## NEXT FRAMEWORK CONVERSATION\n\nThe next conversation starts with:\n\n> **Review and reconcile the existing GCE taxonomy from the top before designing new controls.**\n\nPrimary topics to rediscover/reconcile:\n\n1. Framework layers and authority.\n2. Seats and role boundaries.\n3. Identity vs model vs runtime.\n4. Bootstrap structure.\n5. Header / footer / confidence.\n6. TODO ownership.\n7. Prompt anatomy.\n8. Anchor / checkpoint / commit discipline.\n9. Handoff lifecycle.\n10. Cable Car placement.\n11. Model capability taxonomy, including sub-agents/swarms.\n12. Project/container role.\n13. Only then: Session Controller and Sentinel.\n\nNo redesign should occur until we know which of those controls **already exist, were superseded, or simply stopped being used**.\n\n---\n\n## ANCHOR COMMIT STATUS\n\n**ANCHOR:** `GCE_FRAMEWORK_RECON_ANCHOR_2026-09-25`  \n**STATE:** FROZEN FOR NEXT FRAMEWORK REVIEW  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\nI have anchored the **conversation/session state here**. I have **not** created a literal Git commit on your local machine because this chat does not have direct access to that repository.\n\n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# SESSION FOOTER  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n**SESSION:** GCE Cross-Session Loop Recon / Taxonomy Unwind  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK RECONCILIATION  \n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Anchor complete / ready for Framework taxonomy review  \n\n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**\n\n**VERIFIED / ACCEPTED FOR NEXT SESSION:**  \n- 4-session incident reconciliation complete  \n- Don responsibility corrections captured  \n- Projects/build distinction captured  \n- TODO requirement captured  \n- sub-agent capability requirement captured  \n- Cable Car functional  \n- Coda handoff direction captured  \n- handoff archive lifecycle added to running checklist  \n- Git/worktree discipline added to review list  \n- Session Controller parked  \n\n**UNRESOLVED:**  \n- Existing taxonomy versus proposed changes  \n- Exact bootstrap contents  \n- Existing anchor-commit doctrine and where it dropped out  \n- Universal archive enforcement  \n- final model/runtime capability matrix  \n- Session Controller/Sentinel architecture  \n\n**NEXT ACTION:**  \nStart a clean Framework conversation and reconcile taxonomy from existing accepted Framework state before implementing changes.\n\n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**CANON STATE:** NO CANON CHANGE AUTHORIZED  \n**ANCHOR STATE:** **COMMITTED IN SESSION / READY TO CARRY FORWARD**  \n**SESSION STATUS:** **READY FOR FRAMEWORK CONVERSATION**", "text_chars": 10470, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4b6f18cc-aa9e-472b-8395-2fea02eb2a7b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370331.342, "updated_at": 1790370342.311029, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, I'm— okay, I'm dumping this in here. One thing we got to add to the session footer is the owner. It's hard for Don to scan through because we don't really list it until I get to the devpad. owner who delivered.", "text_chars": 216, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d3e1000d-91bd-4444-ba22-caa439ed9a3c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370333.153865, "updated_at": 1790370333.153865, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b9df9517-9e82-436d-a1f4-febd63c2c8e8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370334.38896, "updated_at": 1790370334.38896, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "69db7482-fc7c-45e0-a713-992b6c6dc70f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370334.391951, "updated_at": 1790370342.16958, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE FRAMEWORK — FOOTER TAXONOMY UPDATE**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK REVIEW  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nAgreed. Add **OWNER** to the session footer as a first-class field.\n\nThe scan problem is real: **PRIMARY SEAT** tells Don who was operating in the session, but it does **not necessarily tell him who owns/delivered the artifact or result**.\n\nSo the footer should distinguish:\n\n- **PRIMARY SEAT** = who is acting in this session\n- **OWNER** = who owns/delivered the current artifact/result\n- **OCCUPANT/MODEL** = optional runtime/model metadata if useful\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nSESSION: GCE Framework Taxonomy Review\nAUTHORITY: Don\nPRIMARY SEAT: Coda\nOWNER: Coda\nOCCUPANT: GPT-5.6 Sol\nMODE: FRAMEWORK REVIEW\nCURRENT PHASE: Taxonomy reconciliation\nCURRENT ARTIFACT: GCE_FRAMEWORK_RECON_ANCHOR_2026-09-25\nCONFIDENCE: 99%\nNEXT ACTION: Review bootstrap taxonomy\nBUILD STATE: NO BUILD AUTHORIZED\nCANON STATE: REVIEW ONLY\nSESSION STATUS: OPEN\n```\n\nAnd when Nico delivers:\n\n```text\nPRIMARY SEAT: Nico\nOWNER: Nico\nOCCUPANT: Space Bunny\n```\n\nWhen Luce verifies Nico's work, the session could be:\n\n```text\nPRIMARY SEAT: Luce\nOWNER: Nico\nVERIFIED BY: Luce\nOCCUPANT: Big Pickle\n```\n\nThat is probably the cleanest distinction because Don can immediately scan:\n\n> **Who owns this thing? Who is currently acting on it? Who verified it?**\n\nI’d add **OWNER** now to the running taxonomy checklist as a required footer field.", "text_chars": 1592, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7c5cc557-5b88-4e2a-8839-806df471f3a6", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370410.847, "updated_at": 1790370422.209944, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, buddy. So what we'll do is go line by line through the session header and session footer. And we'll make 2 separate ones. QuickTime. Versus big build, right? We'll come up with better terminology where we could just toggle in and out of it. You and I.", "text_chars": 257, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a95d6726-c2b2-4b8a-9606-d84ef3a516d8", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370413.246227, "updated_at": 1790370413.246227, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "61f830d1-5060-4b71-ae88-6e152ffae82d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370413.253811, "updated_at": 1790370422.001622, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — that’s the right move.\n\nWe should **not** have one bloated universal header/footer trying to serve both a 10-minute surgical fix and a six-hour build.\n\nWe’ll define **two operating modes** and make them easy to toggle:\n\n- **Quick mode** — small fix, bounded verification, short handoff, minimal ceremony.\n- **Build mode** — substantial implementation, explicit artifact/state/commit/handoff controls.\n\nAnd we’ll go **field by field**, not invent the whole thing in one shot.\n\nThe key is that both still preserve the same identity/control spine:\n\n**Authority → Seat → Owner → Objective → State → Stop → Return**\n\nThe difference is how much operational detail gets exposed.\n\nFor example, Quick mode probably does **not** need a ten-line footer every time. Build mode probably does.\n\nWe’ll also make the terminology cleaner than “QuickTime” versus “big build.” Something like **QUICK / BUILD**, **TACTICAL / BUILD**, or **LIGHT / FULL**—but we’ll decide that when we review the taxonomy rather than naming it prematurely.\n\n### Running item added\n\nWhen we get back to the Framework:\n\n> **Design two standardized session header/footer profiles and a simple mode toggle between them.**\n\nAnd **OWNER** is now explicitly part of that line-by-line review.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Framework review  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**STATUS:** Parked for line-by-line header/footer design  \n**NEXT:** Define Quick-mode header first, then Build-mode header; repeat for footers  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 99%", "text_chars": 1479, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b7b2b0d3-705d-4bd0-8a96-eb198fd6426d", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370439.285, "updated_at": 1790370447.43236, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "The other thing I think I told you last night, we need to do the same thing for prompts, right? The prompts are shorthand versus long, right?", "text_chars": 141, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4231961e-4205-4a71-8042-bb50ff848bc4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790370441.721688, "updated_at": 1790370447.335215, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **Same exact split.**\n\nWe need two prompt profiles:\n\n- **Shorthand prompt** — for bounded, obvious work where the Framework state is already loaded.\n- **Full prompt** — for substantial builds, handoffs, recovery after context loss, or anything crossing seats/runtimes.\n\nThe mistake we made was using **full-constitutional prompts for everything**. That created prompt bloat and made normal debugging feel like governance.\n\nThe shorthand version should probably be closer to:\n\n> **Seat / Objective / Task / Acceptance / Stop / Return**\n\nThe full version can carry the broader frame:\n\n> **Authority / Seat / Owner / Objective / Givens / Context / TODO / Artifact / Constraints / Acceptance / Stop / Handoff / Return**\n\nAnd just like header/footer mode, we should be able to **toggle prompt mode explicitly** rather than improvising each time.\n\nSo yes—add another paired taxonomy item:\n\n**SESSION PROFILE**\n- Quick\n- Build\n\n**PROMPT PROFILE**\n- Shorthand\n- Full\n\nThen we review both line by line and decide what is mandatory versus optional.", "text_chars": 1043, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0f5e5b72-dba8-49c6-baa8-e20c565f8d02", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790372815.482, "updated_at": 1790372843.391597, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "ok good lets look at this LUCE VERIFICATION — NETWORK TRUTH TEST\nFirst: your v1 script produced a false negative. Do not trust that report.\nTwo defects, both now isolated:\n1. Test-Connection -TargetName does not exist in Windows PowerShell 5.1 (correct: -ComputerName). With -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue it swallowed the error and returned nothing → RESULT: NO RESPONSES for all 4 targets. That was the bug, not your network.\n2. | Format-Table/-List | Out-String returns empty in this execution harness — that's why adapters/Wi-Fi/ipconfig/HTTPS/DNS sections were blank. tracert/pathping were also suppressed (unelevated + same pipeline defect).\nI wrote a corrected harness (v2) and ran it. Full 8m33s sweep:\nTarget\n1.1.1.1\n8.8.8.8\ncloudflare.com\n10.8.0.1 (gateway)\nThe 100% gateway result is ICMP filtering, not loss. tracert hop 1 is 10.8.0.3 at 4 ms — first hop is alive and fast. 10.8.0.1 just doesn't answer echo requests. Your own rule 6 predicted exactly this.\nApplication layer (the layer that actually matters for agent streaming): DNS 4/4 resolved (9–28 ms) · TCP 443 30/30 connects, 0 failures (api.openai.com avg 7.5 ms, max 28 ms) · HTTP GET 10/10 → 200 · full trace to 1.1.1.1 in 9 hops, all <20 ms.\nLink: MGMResorts-WiFi, 5 GHz, Wi-Fi 6E, 81% / −62 dBm, 217 Mbps, Open auth / Cipher None (hotel).\n1. VERIFICATION STATUS: Network is NOT the cause. No loss to any internet destination, zero TCP/TLS/HTTP failures, zero DNS failures.\n2. CONFIDENCE: 88%. High on \"network is clean at test time\" (400 ICMP + 30 TCP + 10 HTTP samples). Not 100% — a single 8.5-minute window cannot characterize intermittent hotel-Wi-Fi degradation.\n3. REASON: Gateway anomaly is provably ICMP filtering (tracert contradicts it). Upstream hops all sub-20 ms. The 1/100 to 8.8.8.8 and the 78–123 ms spikes are normal variance, not loss.\n4. EVIDENCE GAP: I have not found the actual cause of the instability. Ruled out: raw loss, DNS, TCP, HTTP. Not ruled out: sustained-vs-bursty behavior during an actual disconnects, Wi-Fi roam/save-sleep, AP-side throttling on long-lived flows, captive-portal/session timeouts, or a client-side/proxy/provider issue. A clean 8-minute sample does not exclude a problem that appears at minute 30.\n5. NEXT VERIFICATION GATE: Correlate against real failure events — run a long-running low-rate monitor (1 pkt/5 s to 1.1.1.1 + gateway bypass 10.8.0.3) and timestamp it against the next observed disconnect. That's the only way to convert \"clean now\" into \"clean during failure.\"\nSTATUS:            PROCEED\nCONFIDENCE:        88%\nOUTCOME:           v1 script defective (2 bugs); v2 corrected run is CLEAN. Network exonerated as primary cause.\nARTIFACTS UPDATED: C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\Desktop\\GCE_LUCE_NET_v2_20260925_140920.txt\nDECISIONS:         None taken - verification only.\nOPEN ITEMS:        Cause of instability NOT identified. Stale misleading v1 report still on Desktop\n                   (GCE_LUCE_NETWORK_TEST_20260925_140329.txt) - recommend deletion, needs your call.\nEXPERTISE NEEDED:  NONE\nNEXT ROUTE:        Don (hold) - or Talon/Keel if you want the long-run monitor stood up.\nANCHOR STATUS:     Anchored to \"network exonerated\"; original anchor (EC8 FIX 1 re-verification) untouched.\nDON INPUT NEEDED:  Delete the v1 report? Stand up the long-duration monitor?\nHANDOFF: NONE\nNEXT ROUTE: Don", "text_chars": 3334, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["Brother\\Desktop\\GCE_LUCE_NET_v2_20260925_140920.txt", "C:\\Users\\Dekan", "GCE_LUCE_NETWORK_TEST_20260925_140329.txt"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "85d543ce-ed18-4314-907d-1b8c75e36656", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790372820.906873, "updated_at": 1790372820.906873, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9fb329c0-1eda-4beb-8466-621aac8ee8f7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790372820.909487, "updated_at": 1790372842.184354, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n**GCE — LUCE NETWORK VERIFICATION REVIEW**  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Luce  \n**MODE:** QUICK REVIEW  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **97%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nThis is a **good Luce return**, and the important first point is mine:\n\n**My v1 PowerShell harness was bad for your environment.** Luce correctly found two concrete implementation defects:\n\n- `Test-Connection -TargetName` is wrong for Windows PowerShell 5.1; it should have used `-ComputerName`.\n- The formatting/output pipeline I used did not survive that execution harness, so major sections of the report were blank.\n\nSo we should **discard the v1 result entirely**. It was not evidence of network failure.\n\n## What Luce actually established\n\nThe corrected v2 evidence is strong enough to say:\n\n> **There is no evidence that packet loss or basic Internet connectivity was causing Space Bunny's instability during the 8.5-minute test window.**\n\nThe application-layer evidence matters most:\n\n- DNS: **4/4 successful**\n- TCP 443: **30/30 successful**\n- HTTP GET: **10/10 HTTP 200**\n- Internet ICMP tests essentially clean\n- Route completed in 9 hops with low latency\n- Wi-Fi signal was respectable at **81% / −62 dBm**\n- negotiated link: **217 Mbps**\n- 5 GHz / Wi-Fi 6E\n\nAnd Luce handled the gateway anomaly correctly. `10.8.0.1` not answering pings does **not** mean 100% gateway packet loss when `10.8.0.3` is visibly responding as the first routed hop at ~4 ms. That is consistent with ICMP filtering.\n\nSo I agree with Luce's **88% confidence** rather than pretending this proves the network is perfect.\n\n## What remains open\n\nThis is now a much narrower problem.\n\nWe have effectively ruled down:\n\n**Not supported as primary cause**\n- persistent raw packet loss;\n- DNS failure;\n- inability to establish TCP;\n- basic HTTP connectivity;\n- generally terrible RF signal at the moment measured.\n\n**Still plausible**\n- intermittent hotel AP behavior;\n- Wi-Fi roaming between APs;\n- power-management/network adapter behavior;\n- captive portal/session renewal;\n- hotel throttling or shaping of long-lived connections;\n- WebSocket/SSE/HTTP2 stream interruption;\n- local proxy/VPN/runtime issue;\n- OpenCode/Space Bunny/provider-side disconnect behavior.\n\nThat last distinction matters because a model session can feel like \"the Internet dropped\" even when ordinary pings and short HTTPS requests stay perfectly healthy.\n\n## Best next test\n\nLuce's next gate is exactly right:\n\n> **Correlate telemetry with an actual Space Bunny failure.**\n\nDo not do another 8-minute broad sweep.\n\nRun a lightweight continuous monitor during real usage and timestamp:\n\n- `1.1.1.1`\n- the responsive first hop `10.8.0.3`\n- possibly periodic TCP 443 to the actual provider endpoint\n\nThen, when Space Bunny freezes/drops, compare the exact timestamp.\n\nThat gives us a very clean fork:\n\n**Network telemetry