e Bunny's active runtime  \n**VALID EVIDENCE:** first-hop + general Internet telemetry only  \n**INVALID FOR SPACE BUNNY:** Bedrock Mantle provider correlation  \n**NEXT ACTION:** Luce grounds the actual Space Bunny/OpenCode provider endpoint and adjusts the existing monitor only for that path  \n**BUILD STATE:** NO BUILD AUTHORIZED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 2003, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b5d87747-b8c9-41e3-9aee-150fcd63ff17", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373296.951, "updated_at": 1790373304.540883, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Fucking A. I, I can't pivot anymore. It must be a project thing. I don't know what's going on. This is horseshit. All I ask is, what is the packet loss? Did he report on packet loss?", "text_chars": 182, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ef90a753-becf-419c-a99e-4b9ed75ef1e5", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373299.305104, "updated_at": 1790373304.311348, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **He did report packet loss.**\n\nFrom Luce’s corrected v2 run:\n\n- **1.1.1.1:** 0% meaningful loss\n- **8.8.8.8:** 1 lost packet out of 100 = **1% loss**\n- **cloudflare.com:** effectively clean\n- **gateway 10.8.0.1:** showed 100% ping “loss,” but Luce explicitly determined that was **ICMP filtering**, not real connectivity loss, because the actual first routed hop at `10.8.0.3` was alive and fast.\n\nHis conclusion was:\n\n> **No evidence of material packet loss during the 8.5-minute test window.**\n\nSo the direct answer to your question is:\n\n**Packet loss was basically zero, with one 1% blip to 8.8.8.8 that Luce considered normal variance, not evidence of a bad connection.**\n\n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**VERDICT:** Packet loss not materially present in the measured window  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 88% on “network clean at test time”", "text_chars": 825, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2735ee11-682e-4442-97c0-f7406a8f5a0a", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373328.485, "updated_at": 1790373344.615036, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, right. All right, did he report? I already did a Wi-Fi connectivity. There's no reason— like, the Wi-Fi at the hotel should be higher than 20 megabytes. Something is wrong, in my opinion. I don't know, something else is cap— something is slowing down this.", "text_chars": 262, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "98902eaa-75ef-4f3c-b210-c8e123384f4d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373336.395947, "updated_at": 1790373336.395947, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1853bffa-f89e-4117-b8b1-94801f9cc1e2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373336.398902, "updated_at": 1790373344.447988, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — and this is the important distinction.\n\nLuce reported the **local Wi-Fi link** at about **217 Mbps**, with **81% signal / −62 dBm** on 5 GHz / Wi-Fi 6E. That means your laptop-to-access-point connection did **not** look like a 20 Mbps radio link.\n\nWhat he **did not report** was an actual end-to-end throughput test showing how much Internet bandwidth MGM was giving you.\n\nSo if your speed test is only around **20–30 Mbps**, while the Wi-Fi link itself is negotiated at **217 Mbps**, then something downstream of the radio could absolutely be constraining throughput: hotel bandwidth shaping, per-device caps, congestion, gateway policy, or upstream capacity.\n\nAnd that fits with the other evidence:\n\n- packet loss: basically clean;\n- signal: decent;\n- local negotiated Wi-Fi rate: much higher than your measured Internet speed;\n- actual Internet throughput: apparently only ~20–30 Mbps.\n\nSo your instinct is reasonable: **this does not look like “your Wi-Fi card can only do 20 Mbps.”** It looks more like the network may be **rate-limited or congested somewhere after the access point**.\n\nIf we want the next clean measurement, it should be one thing only: **Luce measures actual sustained download/upload throughput and compares it to the 217 Mbps link rate.** That would tell us whether we're dealing with a cap versus instability.", "text_chars": 1342, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d0d6e9d3-694c-4b95-9b0f-2fc50f557d5f", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373347.714, "updated_at": 1790373360.892365, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "There we go. Yes, motherfucker!", "text_chars": 31, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "06c204c2-f716-490d-94ca-12df18af0e3d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373358.570734, "updated_at": 1790373360.782474, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. **That’s the right question.**\n\nNot “is Wi-Fi connected?”  \nNot “is there packet loss?”  \nBut:\n\n> **Why is the negotiated Wi-Fi link ~217 Mbps while real Internet throughput is only ~20–30 Mbps?**\n\nThat’s a clean, bounded Luce test.\n\n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**MODE:** Quick verification  \n**NEXT TEST:** Sustained download/upload throughput vs negotiated link rate  \n**GOAL:** Determine whether the bottleneck is hotel shaping/cap, congestion, gateway/upstream, or local client behavior  \n**STOP:** Return the measured throughput and the most likely bottleneck class—no extra architecture, no five-script circus.", "text_chars": 615, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7fd54c68-2568-499a-b0e5-d03e6a3e6c87", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373364.686, "updated_at": 1790373373.898235, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I think it's the fucking Intel Wi-Fi driver.", "text_chars": 44, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7d7494cd-c07d-4f56-8026-16552856a4cd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373367.176245, "updated_at": 1790373373.734651, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Possible — but **not proven yet**.\n\nWhat we have right now actually makes the Intel driver a **reasonable hypothesis**, because:\n\n- negotiated Wi-Fi link is ~217 Mbps;\n- signal is decent;\n- packet loss is basically clean;\n- actual Internet throughput is only ~20–30 Mbps.\n\nThat pattern can come from hotel shaping/congestion, but a bad Intel driver can also cause **poor throughput without obvious packet loss**, especially through:\n- bad power-management behavior,\n- roaming aggressiveness,\n- channel-width negotiation issues,\n- 802.11ax/6E quirks,\n- driver regression,\n- adapter advanced-property settings.\n\nThe clean next move is **not another broad network test**. It’s a Luce quick test comparing:\n\n1. current Intel adapter model + driver version,\n2. current negotiated link rate,\n3. sustained throughput,\n4. adapter advanced settings/power state,\n5. then—only if evidence points there—compare against an alternate known-good driver/settings state.\n\nIf you want, I’ll give you **one compact Luce prompt** for exactly that, no extra crap.", "text_chars": 1042, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dac47231-59a4-4ba3-869b-880ee341fa11", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373391.92, "updated_at": 1790373457.402221, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, so please put your expertise hat on. This needs to be fucking solved in a deep dive. This is crazy because it's capping itself because— not because of the Wi-Fi. I haven't had this problem when I stayed here last time. Like a month ago. Something's wrong with the computer.", "text_chars": 279, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "