st slow” as the default explanation. We should do a **client-side deep dive on the Intel adapter/driver stack**, but prove it before changing anything. One Luce pass, one evidence package, then one surgical fix.", "text_chars": 302, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "558153f9-6a5d-48d0-90b6-93d6d0d76812", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373406.311544, "updated_at": 1790373406.311544, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5d866d38-a734-4dda-a19d-59a13f649cb5", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790373406.415011, "updated_at": 1790373406.415011, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "868e7c53-2e7d-45e7-8331-eb486b80371c", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790373406.578773, "updated_at": 1790373406.578773, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "567b4f59-ce4b-4569-96ec-4002fc808b27", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373409.809241, "updated_at": 1790373409.809241, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f128446e-cdfb-45e4-8444-fac5867565b8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373409.820673, "updated_at": 1790373409.820673, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "539e203f-8293-481a-a50f-064fa7923c6e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373409.825341, "updated_at": 1790373455.504066, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — INTEL WI-FI DEEP DIVE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Luce  \n**MODE:** DEEP VERIFICATION / ROOT-CAUSE  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes. At this point I would treat **client-side Wi-Fi stack regression as a serious hypothesis**, especially because you used this same hotel successfully about a month ago and the machine currently negotiates a much higher Wi-Fi link rate than the ~20–30 Mbps Internet throughput you’re seeing.\n\nIntel itself documents slow Windows Wi-Fi cases tied to driver/configuration problems and recommends checking driver version, clean-install state, wireless mode, roaming, transmit power, and power management. Their recommended AX settings include 5 GHz channel width on Auto, roaming aggressiveness Medium, transmit power Highest, and 802.11ax enabled. citeturn322230search0turn322230search1turn322230search2 Microsoft likewise recommends collecting the adapter model, driver/version/date, BSSID/channel/radio information, recent disconnect history, and WLAN report before changing anything. citeturn322230search3turn322230search4\n\nSo I want **Luce / actual Big Pickle** to own a real forensic pass first. One pass. No random toggling.\n\n:::writing{variant=\"document\" id=\"46281\" title=\"Luce — Intel Wi-Fi Root-Cause Deep Dive\"}\n# GCE LUCE — INTEL WI-FI ROOT-CAUSE DEEP DIVE\n\nAUTHORITY: Don\nSEAT: Luce\nOWNER: Luce\nOCCUPANT: Big Pickle\nMODE: DEEP VERIFICATION / READ-FIRST\nBUILD AUTHORITY: NONE\n\n## OBJECTIVE\n\nDetermine why this Windows machine is achieving only approximately 20–30 Mbps real Internet throughput despite a substantially higher negotiated Wi-Fi link rate and otherwise clean basic connectivity.\n\nDon used this same hotel Wi-Fi successfully approximately one month ago.\n\nDo NOT assume hotel congestion.\nDo NOT assume Intel driver failure.\nProve which layer is constraining throughput.\n\n## CURRENT VERIFIED EVIDENCE\n\n- MGMResorts-WiFi\n- 5 GHz\n- Wi-Fi 6E reported\n- signal approximately 81% / -62 dBm\n- negotiated receive/transmit link previously reported around 217 Mbps\n- corrected Luce test found essentially no material Internet packet loss\n- DNS, TCP 443 and HTTP connectivity were clean during the measured window\n- actual user-observed Internet throughput is roughly 20–30 Mbps\n- prior broad network test does NOT establish why throughput is low\n\n## PRIMARY HYPOTHESES\n\nEvaluate these independently:\n\nA. Intel Wi-Fi driver regression / wrong driver branch\nB. OEM driver vs Intel generic driver mismatch\nC. Windows Update changed driver or network behavior\nD. Intel/Killer connectivity software or traffic-management layer\nE. adapter advanced-setting regression\nF. power-management / PSP behavior\nG. 802.11ax / 6E interoperability issue with hotel AP\nH. channel-width / PHY negotiation issue\nI. roaming/BSSID behavior\nJ. Windows TCP/network-stack limitation\nK. VPN/filter/proxy/security/network-filter driver\nL. hotel-side per-device shaping/congestion\n\nDo not promote any hypothesis without evidence.\n\n## PHASE 1 — IDENTIFY THE CLIENT EXACTLY\n\nCollect, without changing anything:\n\n- exact wireless adapter model\n- PNP/device ID\n- driver provider\n- driver version\n- driver date\n- INF/package identity\n- Windows version/build\n- recent Windows Update driver/update history where obtainable\n- Intel PROSet / Killer / Connectivity Performance Suite / Connectivity Manager presence\n- third-party VPN/filter/security network adapters\n- virtual adapters that may sit in the network path\n\nDetermine whether the current wireless driver is:\n\nOEM-supplied\nIntel generic\nMicrosoft inbox\nor UNKNOWN.\n\n## PHASE 2 — CAPTURE CURRENT RADIO / WLAN STATE\n\nCapture:\n\n- SSID\n- BSSID\n- channel\n- band\n- radio type\n- receive rate\n- transmit rate\n- signal\n- authentication/cipher\n- visible MGM BSSIDs\n- whether the machine appears to roam between BSSIDs\n- supported wireless capabilities\n\nGenerate/read the Windows WLAN report and inspect recent WLAN events for:\n\n- disconnect/reconnect events\n- roaming\n- reason codes\n- driver resets\n- authentication events\n- interface transitions\n\nUse Windows-native evidence where possible.\n\n## PHASE 3 — INSPECT INTEL ADVANCED SETTINGS\n\nRead every current advanced property for the active adapter.\n\nExplicitly identify values for anything equivalent to:\n\n- 802.11n/ac/ax wireless mode\n- channel width for 5 GHz\n- preferred band\n- roaming aggressiveness\n- transmit power\n- throughput booster\n- MIMO power save / SMPS\n- U-APSD\n- packet coalescing\n- fat channel intolerant\n- mixed-mode protection\n- 6 GHz / ultra-high-band setting if exposed\n\nCompare the actual values against Intel's recommended/default AX configuration.\n\nDO NOT CHANGE THEM YET.\n\nFlag each:\n\nNORMAL\nSUSPICIOUS\nUNKNOWN / OEM-SPECIFIC\n\n## PHASE 4 — POWER MANAGEMENT\n\nInspect:\n\n- Windows active power plan\n- wireless adapter power-saving policy on AC and battery\n- Device Manager adapter power-management capability/state where readable\n- PCIe/link-state power management if relevant\n- Modern Standby related behavior if applicable\n\nDetermine whether the adapter may be prioritizing power savings over throughput.\n\nDo not change anything during recon.\n\n## PHASE 5 — WINDOWS NETWORK STACK\n\nInspect, read-only:\n\n- TCP receive-window autotuning state\n- RSS state\n- RSC state where applicable\n- ECN state\n- MTU\n- interface metrics\n- proxy state\n- WinHTTP proxy\n- DNS configuration\n- active routes\n- VPN/tunnel/filter adapters\n- QoS policies if present\n\nFlag abnormal or non-default state.\n\nDo NOT perform global `netsh int ip reset`, Winsock reset, DNS flushes, registry tweaks, or shotgun network resets.\n\n## PHASE 6 — THROUGHPUT CHARACTERIZATION\n\nRun controlled repeated throughput measurements.\n\nWe need to distinguish:\n\nPHY RATE\nfrom\nREAL INTERNET THROUGHPUT.\n\nMeasure multiple runs rather than one screenshot.\n\nRecord:\n\n- download Mbps\n- upload Mbps\n- latency\n- negotiated RX/TX link rate at the same moment\n- BSSID/channel at the same moment\n\nIf an approved existing speed-test utility is already installed, use it.\n\nDo NOT download random benchmarking software merely to complete this test.\n\nAlso test whether throughput changes materially across several consecutive runs.\n\nLook for a suspicious ceiling such as:\n\n~20 Mbps every run\n~25 Mbps every run\n~30 Mbps every run\n\nA repeatable hard ceiling is different evidence from variable hotel congestion.\n\n## PHASE 7 — COMPARATIVE EVIDENCE\n\nIf another device Don has available on the same MGM network can be compared without disrupting work, record its speed as CONTROL DEVICE.\n\nSame approximate location.\nSame Wi-Fi.\nSame period.\n\nDo not require this if unavailable.\n\nInterpretation:\n\nCONTROL FAST + WINDOWS MACHINE SLOW\nstrong evidence toward client.\n\nBOTH SLOW\nstronger evidence toward hotel/network.\n\n## PHASE 8 — DRIVER HISTORY\n\nDetermine whether the Intel Wi-Fi driver changed since Don's last successful stay if evidence exists locally.\n\nCheck:\n\n- installed driver history\n- Windows Update history\n- driver package timestamps\n- available rollback metadata\n\nDo NOT roll back or upgrade yet.\n\nIf possible, identify the immediately previous driver version and current recommended OEM/Intel versions.\n\n## REQUIRED ROOT-CAUSE RETURN\n\nReturn a ranked evidence table:\n\nCAUSE\nEVIDENCE FOR\nEVIDENCE AGAINST\nCONFIDENCE\nNEXT PROOF\n\nThen classify the current bottleneck as exactly one:\n\nCLIENT_DRIVER\nCLIENT_CONFIGURATION\nCLIENT_POWER_MANAGEMENT\nCLIENT_NETWORK_STACK\nHOTEL_CAP_OR_SHAPING\nHOTEL_CONGESTION\nRADIO_INTEROP\nMIXED\nINCONCLUSIVE\n\n## SURGICAL-FIX GATE\n\nOnly after the evidence return, recommend the smallest reversible fix.\n\nPreferred order if evidence supports client-side fault:\n\n1. correct one clearly wrong adapter setting;\n2. correct power policy;\n3. disable/remove proven traffic-management interference;\n4. driver rollback if regression evidence exists;\n5. driver update/clean installation if current package is suspect.\n\nDo not start by uninstalling the adapter.\nDo not shotgun multiple settings.\nDo not change driver + power + AX mode simultaneously.\n\nWe need before/after attribution.\n\n## ACCEPTANCE\n\nThe investigation is successful only if it gives Don:\n\n- exact adapter and driver state;\n- real PHY rate;\n- real sustained throughput;\n- meaningful client-vs-hotel evidence;\n- suspicious configuration findings;\n- driver-history evidence where available;\n- one recommended next change, not five speculative fixes.\n\n## STOP\n\nStop after the forensic report.\n\nDo not modify the Wi-Fi configuration until Don/Coda reviews the evidence.\n\n## RETURN\n\nSTATUS:\nOWNER: Luce\nADAPTER:\nCURRENT DRIVER:\nDRIVER DATE:\nDRIVER SOURCE:\nWINDOWS BUILD:\nPHY RATE:\nMEASURED DOWNLOAD:\nMEASURED UPLOAD:\nCONTROL DEVICE RESULT:\nSUSPICIOUS SETTINGS:\nDRIVER HISTORY:\nROOT-CAUSE CLASS:\nCONFIDENCE:\nONE RECOMMENDED NEXT CHANGE:\nROLLBACK PATH:\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nNEXT ROUTE: Don / Coda\n:::\n\nThat is the right depth. **First prove whether the computer is the bottleneck. Then we touch exactly one thing.**\n\nAnd there’s a useful tell here: if Luce finds the laptop repeatedly negotiating ~200+ Mbps while its Internet throughput hits nearly the **same 20–30 Mbps ceiling every run**, and another device on the same Wi-Fi materially exceeds it, the evidence swings strongly toward the client machine rather than MGM. Conversely, if every device hits the same ceiling, we stop blaming Intel.\n\n**Do not have Nico fix anything yet. Luce owns truth first.**\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Deep verification  \n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**PRIMARY HYPOTHESIS:** Client Wi-Fi stack/driver regression — unproven  \n**ACCEPTANCE:** Client vs hotel bottleneck isolated with evidence  \n**NEXT:** One forensic pass → one surgical change  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96% on methodology**", "text_chars": 9776, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f179da81-4fe3-4754-b286-1e9b3b73c458", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373475.518, "updated_at": 1790373486.363798, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes, and again Yes, and again, I've gone from multiple hotels, Excalibur and Luxor. It stays the same cap, 20 megabytes, 25 megabytes. You just found out it's 217. Don't you see the problem? Right, hypothetically.", "text_chars": 213, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7813b9f9-4717-4b6e-accf-c7016b754d03", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373477.790501, "updated_at": 1790373486.146438, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — **hypothetically, that pattern is a big red flag for the client machine**, not the individual hotel.\n\nYou now have this pattern across **multiple separate hotel networks**:\n\n- Excalibur: ~20–25 Mbps\n- Luxor: ~20–25 Mbps\n- current MGM network: same general ceiling\n- meanwhile the laptop’s Wi-Fi link is negotiating around **217 Mbps**\n\nThat does **not prove** the Intel driver is the cause, but it absolutely changes the prior.\n\nA repeatable ceiling following the **same laptop across different networks** is much more consistent with something local to the machine than with three different hotel networks all independently throttling you to roughly the same number.\n\nThe main client-side suspects become:\n\n- Intel driver regression\n- adapter advanced setting limiting throughput\n- power-management state\n- OEM/Killer traffic management\n- VPN/filter/network shim\n- Windows network-stack issue\n- some software-level bandwidth cap or QoS behavior\n\nThe 217 Mbps number matters because it says the radio link itself is capable of far more than 20–25 Mbps at that moment. So the bottleneck is likely happening **above the raw Wi-Fi link layer**.\n\nThe clean proof would be:\n\n> same laptop + different networks = same ~20–25 Mbps ceiling  \n> another device on those same networks = materially faster\n\nIf that holds, then the client-side case becomes very strong.\n\nSo yes — **I see the problem. The repeated cap moving with the machine is the important clue.**\n\n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**CURRENT HYPOTHESIS:** Client-side throughput ceiling  \n**CONFIDENCE:** High that the pattern is suspicious; root cause still unproven", "text_chars": 1614, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1198fc5c-5e0e-4b9d-8d11-c352c29aa354", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373515.821, "updated_at": 1790373526.947641, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes, on top of that, I've already tried to basically reset the network. Update the driver, but that was on a different card. I don't think Luce did it right. Something's fucked up.", "text_chars": 180, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3369cd59-ba5e-4987-a2fa-e0c3cd359be4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373518.503325, "updated_at": 1790373526.812823, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — with that history, I would **not** trust a generic “reset network / update driver” as proof that the client stack is clean.\n\nWhat matters is **which card, which driver package, which settings, and whether the problem followed the machine before and after that change**.\n\nA reset can leave behind the actual limiter if the culprit is:\n\n- a different Intel adapter than the one that got updated,\n- an OEM-vs-Intel driver mismatch,\n- Killer/Intel connectivity software,\n- a filter driver or VPN shim,\n- power-management settings,\n- a bad advanced-property value,\n- Windows choosing the wrong driver branch after reinstall,\n- or a repeatable software cap above the radio layer.\n\nSo I agree with your instinct: **something local to the machine still looks wrong**, and Luce’s first pass was too broad to settle that.\n\nThe next return from Luce should explicitly answer four things before touching anything else:\n\n1. **Exact active Wi-Fi card**  \n2. **Exact current driver/version/provider/date for that card**  \n3. **What driver you previously updated/reset, and whether it was the same device**  \n4. **What software/filter layers sit between Windows and the network**\n\nIf the “driver update” was on a different adapter, then it tells us almost nothing about this current problem.\n\n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**CURRENT HYPOTHESIS:** client-side throughput limiter remains likely  \n**NEXT:** identify the exact active Intel card and its actual driver/software stack before any further reset  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 97% on the diagnostic direction", "text_chars": 1532, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2bc67a99-d3ae-47db-8bd2-1812245eff29", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373552.678, "updated_at": 1790373570.908246, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Perfect. We'll let him do it, and then let's go through this because this is ridiculous. I've never been on a Wi-Fi in a hotel that's 20 megabytes. Maybe my— again, we switch an IP. Maybe mine got fucking— whatever's on my computer from the MGM internet knows we're running a bunch of AI models, so they cap Don. Maybe we refresh it.", "text_chars": 333, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3c34387d-c1ec-409c-b669-f0c5f87bb0c7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373557.186771, "updated_at": 1790373557.186771, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "35440d8c-1270-4fe0-8482-da3110c53ecd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373557.189434, "updated_at": 1790373570.66443, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — WIFI ROOT-CAUSE HYPOTHESIS REVIEW  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** QUICK RECON  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes — **refreshing the client identity on the hotel network is a legitimate hypothesis**, but I would separate that from the idea that MGM somehow “knows Don is running AI models.”\n\nThe more plausible network-side version is:\n\n> **The hotel has associated your device identity with a captive-portal session, QoS class, stale lease, or per-device bandwidth profile.**\n\nHotels commonly key that sort of thing off combinations of:\n- MAC address / device identity,\n- captive-portal session,\n- DHCP lease,\n- IP assignment,\n- sometimes device fingerprinting or traffic class.\n\nSo hypothetically, if the **same laptop** keeps getting treated the same way across MGM properties, a stale or persistent client identity could contribute.\n\nBut the part I would **not** assume is:\n\n> “They detected AI use and capped me.”\n\nThat is possible in a generic traffic-management sense if they shape certain long-lived or high-volume traffic classes, but we have **no evidence** of AI-specific throttling.\n\n### The clean test\n\nAfter Luce finishes the client-side forensic pass, there is a very simple network-identity experiment:\n\n1. Record current:\n   - MAC/private-address state\n   - IP\n   - BSSID\n   - throughput\n2. Forget/rejoin the MGM network or deliberately rotate the private Wi-Fi address **once**.\n3. Obtain a fresh DHCP/captive-portal session.\n4. Re-run the exact same throughput test.\n\nIf speed suddenly jumps from ~20–25 Mbps to something materially higher, that is strong evidence of **per-device/session shaping or stale network identity**.\n\nIf it stays capped at the same number, we move back toward:\n- client driver/settings,\n- local filter/QoS layer,\n- or an MGM-wide policy.\n\nThat test is useful because it changes **one variable only**: network identity/session.\n\n### My current hierarchy\n\nGiven everything you’ve told me, I’d rank the hypotheses like this:\n\n**High**\n- client-side driver/config/filter/power issue\n- persistent per-device/session shaping\n\n**Medium**\n- MGM-wide per-device cap\n- hotel QoS policy affecting your traffic profile\n\n**Lower**\n- raw Wi-Fi signal problem\n- packet loss\n- DNS\n- “MGM specifically detected AI and throttled Don”\n\nSo yes: **refreshing the device/session identity is absolutely worth testing**, but only after Luce captures the current state so we don’t destroy the evidence.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Quick recon  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**PRIMARY HYPOTHESES:** Client stack vs persistent per-device/session shaping  \n**NEXT:** Let Luce finish the forensic capture, then perform one controlled identity/session refresh test  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **96%**", "text_chars": 2879, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "74f256b5-759e-42de-a4e4-9a38806fb067", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373598.118, "updated_at": 1790373608.376734, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, I'm just— yeah, thank you. You can keep in your lane. Exactly. I think it just fucking keyed Don in and capped Don. I could do a test with my hotspot on my new phone, but I'd rather just try to figure this out now. Right, we have enough root causes and enough hypothesis. We should be able— especially since you said 217 megabytes. I've never ever in my history of staying in an MGM hotel been this slow.", "text_chars": 410, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "72414d7e-c510-488b-ab4e-cc4f4a0debba", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790373600.451, "updated_at": 1790373608.265334, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. We have enough signal now that we should **stop inventing new theories** and test the short list cleanly.\n\nThe strongest clue is still this pattern:\n\n- same laptop,\n- multiple MGM properties,\n- roughly the same **20–25 Mbps ceiling**,\n- while the Wi-Fi link itself is negotiating around **217 Mbps**,\n- and basic packet loss/DNS/TCP looked clean during testing.\n\nThat makes this a **throughput bottleneck problem**, not a basic connectivity problem.\n\nThe practical order should be:\n\n1. **Finish Luce’s client-side forensic pass**  \n   Exact adapter, exact driver, advanced properties, power policy, filters/VPN/Killer/Intel software, Windows network stack.\n\n2. **Then do one controlled network-identity refresh test**  \n   Capture current state first, then refresh the device/session identity on MGM Wi-Fi and rerun the same throughput measurement.\n\n3. **Only after that**, if the cap remains, use the phone hotspot as the control network.  \n   If the laptop suddenly performs normally on the hotspot, that heavily implicates MGM/session-side behavior.  \n   If it still caps around 20–25 Mbps, the machine becomes the dominant suspect.\n\nThat three-step sequence should collapse this pretty fast.\n\nAnd yes, the **217 Mbps negotiated link** is exactly why the current 20–25 Mbps ceiling deserves scrutiny. It does not prove the laptop should get 217 Mbps Internet throughput, but it does show the local radio link is not inherently operating at a 20 Mbps PHY rate.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Quick recon  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**PRIMARY ISSUE:** Persistent ~20–25 Mbps throughput ceiling across MGM networks  \n**NEXT:** Luce client-stack forensics → one identity/session refresh → hotspot control only if needed  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 97% on diagnostic sequence", "text_chars": 1757, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "36ec0659-c47b-497d-b018-47f3bb25a4f2", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376305.269, "updated_at": 1790376326.935026, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "LUCE FORENSIC REPORT — Wi-Fi THROUGHPUT ROOT CAUSE\nNothing was modified. Read-only throughout. (One stray artifact: Get-WindowsUpdateLog was triggered unintentionally and wrote C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\Desktop\\WindowsUpdate.log. No system state touched.)\nTHE DECISIVE EVIDENCE\nThroughput is pinned at ~19 Mbps and refuses to move — invariant to concurrency and direction:\nRun\tStreams\tDirection\n1\t1\tdown\n2\t8\tdown\n3\t16\tdown\n4–5\t1\tup\nSpread across 7 runs / 6 minutes: 18.44–19.27 Mbps = ±2%. Meanwhile PHY link stayed 229 Mbps, signal 82% / −59 dBm, 0% loss over 400+ packets.\n16 parallel streams produced zero gain over 1