NOT OEM, NOT MS inbox. Inferred: original 2019 card was AC 9560\nWINDOWS BUILD:     Windows 11 Home 25H2, 10.0.26200.9550\nPHY RATE:          229 Mbps rx/tx @ 5GHz ch52, 802.11ax, BSSID 88:9c:ad:c9:c9:ce,\n                   signal 82% / -59 dBm, Open/None. (Drops to 143-206 under load =\n                   normal rate adaptation, not a fault)\nMEASURED DOWNLOAD: 19.04 / 18.99 / 19.20 Mbps  (1 / 8 / 16 streams)\nMEASURED UPLOAD:   19.27 / 18.44 Mbps\nCONTROL DEVICE:    NOT OBTAINED - REQUIRES DON. This is the one blocking gap.\nSUSPICIOUS SETTINGS: NONE. Every advanced setting is Intel default or best-case:\n                   MIMO PS=No SMPS | TxPower=Highest | Mode=802.11ax | Width=Auto\n                   U-APSD=Disabled | Roaming=Medium | Band=Prefer 5GHz.\n                   TCP stack 100% default. No proxy, no VPN adapter, no filter binding.\nDRIVER HISTORY:    Store holds oem67.inf + oem7.inf (both 24.40.0.4, 04/13/2026) and\n                   oem42.inf (20.120.1.1, 02/13/2019 - original card, rollback candidate).\n                   NO driver install events in 45 days of Kernel-PnP.\n                   => Driver did NOT change since Don's last good stay. RULES OUT C.\nROOT-CAUSE CLASS:  HOTEL_CAP_OR_SHAPING\nCONFIDENCE:        75% hotel-side (client excluded at ~92%); 55% on cap-vs-congestion\n                   specifically -- those two I could not split without a control device.\nONE RECOMMENDED NEXT CHANGE:\n                   *** NO CLIENT CHANGE. NONE IS EVIDENCE-SUPPORTED. ***\n                   Run the CONTROL-DEVICE test: a phone on MGMResorts-WiFi, same spot,\n                   same time, run any browser speed test. Report the number to me.\n                     - Control also ~19 Mbps  => hotel confirmed, STOP, escalate to\n                       hotel/ISP. No client work warranted at any point.\n                     - Control fast (>100 Mbps) => client fault re-opens; the single\n                       first reversible change would be Throughput Booster\n                       Disabled -> Enabled (one setting, instant revert), measured\n                       before/after. Not before that.\nROLLBACK PATH:     Contiguous setting only: Throughput Booster -> Disabled.\n                   Driver rollback: oem42.inf (20.120.1.1) present, but built for the\n                   older AC 9560 -- do NOT attempt without a fresh evidence case.\nEVIDENCE GAPS:     1. NO CONTROL DEVICE (Phase 7) - highest-value missing proof.\n                   2. NO SECOND NETWORK OPERATOR VALIDATED. Hetzner TLS-failed,\n                      Level3 403, SoftLayer/Panama errored. The ~19 Mbps ceiling is\n                      confirmed against Cloudflare's anycast edge ONLY. A cap specific\n                      to that edge path is not formally excluded.\n                   3. wlanreport + WFP filter dump REQUIRE ELEVATION - not attempted.\n                      Wi-Fi reason codes / driver-reset history unverified beyond the\n                      24h AutoConfig log (which was clean: 1 assoc, 0 disconnects).\n                   4. Single ~6 min window. Hotel load varies by hour; not sampled.\n                   5. Negotiated channel WIDTH is not exposed by netsh on this build.\n                      229 Mbps is consistent with 802.11ax 20 MHz 2SS, implying the AP\n                      may be serving 20 MHz - but that is NOT the throughput cause\n                      (229 PHY vs 19 goodput). Flagged, not concluded.\nNEXT ROUTE:        Don / Coda\nTWO THINGS THAT NEED YOUR ATTENTION\nThe stall monitor (PID 12068) is still running, but its samples from ~14:59–15:05 are invalid for stall correlation — the throughput tests saturated the link. Tell me whether to stop it or keep it armed.\nThis exonerates the network as the cause of the Space Bunny stalls for throughput — but note the irony: the earlier stall question and this one have different answers. The link is clean and fast-enough at the IP layer; the ~19 Mbps cap is a bandwidth limit, not a stability limit. It does not by itself explain disconnects.\n▣  Build · Big Pickle · 9m 16s", "text_chars": 6623, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["Brother\\Desktop\\WindowsUpdate.log", "C:\\Users\\Dekan"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "8466fc62-8d2c-4d93-8c8c-14cd1da7d1bd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376307.601615, "updated_at": 1790376307.601615, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "13542cde-0164-4c90-b621-650abc85a33b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376309.349425, "updated_at": 1790376309.349425, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6df2e3e9-7984-4c23-b63b-c5870e6668c6", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376309.352463, "updated_at": 1790376326.545477, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — LUCE WIFI FORENSIC REVIEW  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Luce  \n**MODE:** DEEP VERIFICATION REVIEW  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes — **this is the first report that actually nails the shape of the problem.**\n\nThe decisive part is not just “19 Mbps.” It’s this:\n\n> **1 stream = ~19 Mbps**  \n> **8 streams = ~19 Mbps**  \n> **16 streams = ~19 Mbps**  \n> **upload = ~19 Mbps too**\n\nThat is a **hard ceiling**, not normal Wi-Fi slowness.\n\nAnd with the radio still negotiating around **229 Mbps**, signal ~82%, essentially no loss, sane Intel settings, default Windows TCP stack, no active VPN/filter binding, and no recent driver change, Luce has done a good job pushing the client machine way down the list.\n\n## What this means\n\nThe evidence now strongly favors one of two upstream explanations:\n\n**1. Per-client shaping/cap**  \nSomething in the MGM network is limiting this device/session to ~19 Mbps in both directions.\n\n**2. Hotel/WAN policy or congestion**  \nLess likely because true congestion usually breathes more; it doesn’t normally sit at an eerily flat ±2% ceiling across 1, 8, and 16 streams.\n\nThat’s why I agree with Luce putting **per-client cap above generic congestion**.\n\nAnd the most important thing he found relative to your earlier instinct is:\n\n> **The laptop itself is not behaving like a 20 Mbps radio.**\n\nThe **229 Mbps PHY** and the invariance across parallel streams are the smoking gun that the bottleneck is higher up the stack.\n\n### One correction to Luce's wording\n\nI would not say this “eliminates every client-side explanation” with absolute certainty. That’s a little too strong. But it does make the common ones—driver, TCP windowing, power policy, radio quality, simple Windows stack bottleneck—**very unlikely**.\n\n## The next proof is dead simple\n\nDo exactly one control test:\n\n**Put your new phone on the same MGMResorts-WiFi, same room, same time, run a browser speed test.**\n\nThen:\n\n- **Phone also ~19 Mbps** → hotel/network policy confirmed strongly.\n- **Phone materially faster** → this device/session is being treated differently, and then your “Don got keyed/capped” hypothesis becomes much more plausible in a generic per-device/session-shaping sense.\n\nThat is the highest-value next move by far.\n\n## What I would *not* do yet\n\nDo **not**:\n- roll back Intel drivers,\n- enable Throughput Booster,\n- reset Windows networking again,\n- touch VPN/filter stack,\n- change power settings,\n- uninstall adapters.\n\nLuce’s report gives us no evidence those are the bottleneck.\n\n## One more important split\n\nLuce is also right that this **19 Mbps cap and the Space Bunny stalls are separate problems**.\n\nA symmetric 19 Mbps cap is annoying, but 19 Mbps is still plenty for terminal/model streaming. So the cap probably explains “why the hotel feels absurdly slow,” but **not necessarily why Space Bunny stalls/disconnects**.\n\nSo we now have two lanes:\n\n**Lane A — throughput cap**  \nAlmost certainly upstream of the client; control device test next.\n\n**Lane B — Space Bunny stability**  \nStill needs event correlation; don’t conflate them.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Deep verification review  \n**OWNER:** Luce  \n**VERDICT:** Strong evidence for upstream bandwidth shaping/cap  \n**CLIENT DRIVER:** low-probability based on current evidence  \n**NEXT PROOF:** Same-SSID control-device speed test  \n**DO NOT CHANGE CLIENT:** correct  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% on evidence interpretation / ~75% on hotel-side class / ~55% cap-vs-congestion split**", "text_chars": 3640, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ccb68a24-6b79-4cfd-a3a2-8c5f8d4ec6ba", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376373.273, "updated_at": 1790376389.168708, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "This pisses Don off. Luce tells Don it's 2:17, and now you're coming back that it's just congestion. If it was just congestion, when I'm working at 4 in the morning, it should alleviate. It stays the same.", "text_chars": 205, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5700dad3-c674-47d3-9ae1-803ef212928b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376377.039616, "updated_at": 1790376377.039616, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4eec07e2-0f86-4274-8209-995dd335eaac", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376377.042654, "updated_at": 1790376388.911495, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — CORRECTION ON WIFI ROOT CAUSE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** QUICK RECON  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYou’re right. I softened Luce’s result too much.\n\n**The evidence does *not* look like ordinary congestion.** Luce’s own ranking had **hotel per-client cap #1** and oversubscribed WAN #2, and the time-of-day behavior you just added makes generic congestion even weaker.\n\nThe key pattern is:\n\n- PHY link around **217–229 Mbps**\n- real throughput pinned around **19 Mbps**\n- 1 stream, 8 streams, 16 streams: basically the same\n- upload also around **19 Mbps**\n- multiple MGM properties\n- same ceiling late at night / around **4 AM**\n- low packet loss\n- clean signal/radio state\n\nThat is much more consistent with a **policy/shaping ceiling** than with crowd congestion.\n\nIf it were ordinary hotel congestion, I would expect at least some of these to move:\n- speed materially higher at 4 AM,\n- more variance run to run,\n- parallel streams occasionally breaking above the ceiling,\n- upload/download behaving differently,\n- latency/bufferbloat worsening under load.\n\nInstead, Luce saw an almost mechanical cap around 19 Mbps.\n\nSo the working hypothesis should be:\n\n> **MGM-side per-device / per-session / policy shaping is now the leading explanation.**\n\nNot “the hotel is busy.”\n\nThe remaining proof is still the same simple control:\n**same room, same MGM Wi-Fi, different device.**\n\nIf your phone gets, say, 80–150+ Mbps while the laptop stays at ~19, then we have very strong evidence the laptop/device identity is being treated differently by the network.\n\nIf the phone also lands at ~19, then it’s probably a network-wide policy cap rather than device-specific shaping.\n\nBut yes — your 4 AM observation matters a lot. It pushes **congestion down** and **intentional shaping/cap up**.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT MODE:** Quick recon  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**LEADING HYPOTHESIS:** MGM per-device/per-session bandwidth shaping  \n**CONGESTION:** now secondary / weak fit  \n**NEXT PROOF:** Same-SSID control device  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99% on interpretation of the evidence pattern**", "text_chars": 2256, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3b9b011e-1cf4-4d18-b07e-5525dd8f5dbb", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376405.313, "updated_at": 1790376425.816703, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, so we— I think it's capping. Find a fucking way around this. Because again, I think I just have to reset my