[], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f6df8030-842e-42a7-8ce1-fe5fbf8cd76d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376410.851963, "updated_at": 1790376410.851963, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ad47e97a-5566-4ed0-bfc0-83b28571c20b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376410.855165, "updated_at": 1790376425.442981, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — MGM THROUGHPUT CAP BYPASS PLAN  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** QUICK TECHNICAL PLAN  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **97%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYes. If this is **per-device/session shaping**, the most likely “reset the ports or whatever” thing you’re reaching for is really a **client identity/session refresh**, not a literal TCP-port reset.\n\nPublic/hotel Wi-Fi commonly uses captive-portal and guest-access systems that can key policy to a device/session identity, including MAC-authenticated guest access and captive-portal state. citeturn660151search1turn660151search7\n\nSo the cleanest way around a sticky per-device cap is to **change only the network identity**, then re-authenticate and retest.\n\n## Do this in this order\n\n1. **Capture current state first**\n   - current Wi-Fi MAC\n   - current IP\n   - current BSSID\n   - current ~19 Mbps result\n\n2. **Forget the MGM Wi-Fi profile**\n   - disconnect\n   - forget network\n\n3. **Change the private/randomized MAC for that SSID**\n   - Windows 11 supports randomized hardware addresses on Wi-Fi networks.\n   - Rejoin as what the network sees as a new client.\n\n4. **Force a fresh captive-portal session**\n   - reconnect to MGM Wi-Fi\n   - open a plain HTTP page if necessary so the captive portal reappears\n   - complete the login/terms flow again\n\n5. **Renew IP state**\n   - fresh DHCP lease comes naturally on reconnect; if not, use `ipconfig /release` and `ipconfig /renew`\n\n6. **Immediately rerun the exact same throughput test**\n   - same room\n   - same laptop\n   - same speed test\n   - compare directly against ~19 Mbps\n\n### Interpretation\n\n- **Jumps materially above ~19 Mbps** → strong evidence the old device/session identity was being shaped.\n- **Still exactly ~19 Mbps** → likely MGM policy on that network class, or the shaping keys on more than MAC/IP.\n- **Phone is much faster on same SSID while laptop stays ~19** → reinforces device-specific treatment.\n- **Phone also ~19** → likely network-wide cap, not Don-specific.\n\nI would **not** reset Winsock, TCP/IP, or reinstall drivers right now. Luce’s forensic work already pushed those way down the list, and those changes would muddy attribution.\n\nAnd importantly, this is not “bypassing security” in the exploit sense; it’s a normal client-side reconnection/identity refresh on a guest network you’re authorized to use. Windows and public Wi-Fi systems are explicitly designed around captive-portal reauthentication and device/session handling. citeturn660151search0turn660151search1\n\nIf you want, I can give you **one single PowerShell block** that captures the current state, forgets the SSID, rotates/reconnects the client identity where Windows allows it, renews DHCP, and then tells you exactly what to test next.", "text_chars": 2899, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "660da718-4733-4253-ab20-6f0848c5e457", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376436.829, "updated_at": 1790376488.699169, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, I've had enough of you and Luce. Give Don a full pass. for Space Bunny or GLM or somebody who wants to help Don.", "text_chars": 118, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0d365937-ffe4-4547-afdb-7b4094b65d55", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376444.785562, "updated_at": 1790376444.785562, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1cc60212-0ee5-4657-974a-52e5ea182328", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376444.789583, "updated_at": 1790376495.61187, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE — INDEPENDENT WIFI / SPACE BUNNY DEEP DIVE  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don  \n**REQUESTING SEAT:** Coda  \n**OWNER:** Independent Technical Investigator  \n**MODE:** FULL PASS / ROOT-CAUSE / FIX  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 99% on scope  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nGive this to **Space Bunny or GLM**. I want them to ignore our prior conclusions and do a genuinely independent pass.\n\n```text\nGCE INDEPENDENT TECHNICAL PASS — WINDOWS WIFI THROUGHPUT + SPACE BUNNY STABILITY\n\nAUTHORITY: Don\nREQUESTOR: Coda\nOWNER: You, independent technical investigator\nMODE: DEEP ROOT-CAUSE / FIX\nBUILD AUTHORITY: BOUNDED SYSTEM DIAGNOSTIC ONLY\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nDon’s Windows laptop is repeatedly capped at approximately 19–25 Mbps on multiple MGM hotel Wi-Fi networks despite the Wi-Fi radio negotiating roughly 217–229 Mbps.\n\nSeparately, Space Bunny/OpenCode sessions have been stalling and disrupting Don’s workflow.\n\nYour job is to independently determine:\n\n1. why real throughput is pinned near 19 Mbps;\n2. whether that limit is client-side, device/session-specific hotel shaping, or network-wide policy;\n3. whether Space Bunny stalls are related or a separate issue;\n4. the smallest reversible fix that should actually be tried.\n\nDo not accept Luce’s or Coda’s previous conclusions automatically.\n\nChallenge the evidence.\n\n============================================================\nKNOWN EVIDENCE\n============================================================\n\nHARD OBSERVATIONS\n\n- Same laptop has shown approximately 20–25 Mbps at:\n  - Excalibur\n  - Luxor\n  - current MGM stay\n- Don reports this persists even at very low-demand hours such as approximately 4 AM.\n- Don has stayed on MGM Wi-Fi previously without this unusually low ceiling.\n- Current WLAN:\n  - Intel Wi-Fi 6E AX210 160 MHz\n  - 5 GHz\n  - 802.11ax\n  - signal approximately 82% / -59 dBm\n  - negotiated PHY approximately 217–229 Mbps rx/tx\n- Basic connectivity testing showed:\n  - essentially no meaningful packet loss\n  - DNS clean\n  - TCP connectivity clean\n  - HTTP connectivity clean\n\nLUCE THROUGHPUT RESULTS\n\nDownload:\n- 1 stream: 19.04 Mbps\n- 8 streams: 18.99 Mbps\n- 16 streams: 19.20 Mbps\n\nUpload:\n- 19.27 Mbps\n- 18.44 Mbps\n\nObserved spread across approximately 7 runs:\n18.44–19.27 Mbps\n\nThe ceiling is therefore unusually flat and symmetric.\n\nCURRENT CLIENT DETAILS\n\nAdapter:\nIntel(R) Wi-Fi 6E AX210 160MHz\nPCI\\VEN_8086&DEV_2725&SUBSYS_00248086&REV_1A\n\nMachine:\nRazer Blade 15 Mid 2019 Base\n\nDriver:\n24.40.0.4\nINF: oem67.inf\nDate: 2026-04-13\nSource: Intel generic, not Razer OEM\n\nOther findings:\n- no recent Wi-Fi driver change in approximately 45 days\n- adapter settings looked generally sane:\n  - MIMO power save: No SMPS\n  - transmit power: Highest\n  - 802.11ax enabled\n  - width Auto\n  - U-APSD disabled\n  - roaming Medium\n  - Prefer 5 GHz\n- Windows TCP stack reportedly default\n- no active proxy\n- ProtonVPN services stopped\n- no active VPN adapter bound to Wi-Fi\n- dormant old WireGuard drivers exist\n- no obvious active filter binding found\n- power state reportedly normal\n- packet loss essentially zero during tests\n\nIMPORTANT:\nLuce tested a Bedrock Mantle endpoint during a previous network monitor.\nSPACE BUNNY DOES NOT USE BEDROCK MANTLE.\nDo not use Bedrock Mantle as the Space Bunny provider path.\n\n============================================================\nIMPORTANT DON CONTEXT\n============================================================\n\nDon suspects this may be a device/session cap or stale network identity.\n\nPossibilities include:\n\n- captive portal session\n- DHCP/session state\n- per-device MAC-based policy\n- QoS profile\n- persistent client identity\n- Windows private/randomized MAC state\n- stale guest network session\n- Intel driver/configuration\n- filter driver\n- traffic-management software\n- Windows network stack issue\n- hotel-wide bandwidth policy\n\nDon has already done generic “network reset” style troubleshooting in the past.\n\nDo not repeat broad resets without evidence.\n\nDon does not want five unrelated PowerShell scripts.\n\nPrefer:\nONE coherent diagnostic/fix pass.\n\n============================================================\nFIRST PRINCIPLE\n============================================================\n\nSeparate these two problems:\n\nA. THROUGHPUT CAP\napproximately 19 Mbps symmetric ceiling.\n\nB. SPACE BUNNY STALLS\nruntime/model session instability.\n\nThey may share a cause.\nThey may be completely independent.\n\nDo not conflate them.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 1 — INDEPENDENT CLIENT AUDIT\n============================================================\n\nVerify the active client state yourself.\n\nCheck:\n\n- exact Wi-Fi adapter and driver\n- driver provider/version/date\n- OEM vs Intel generic driver\n- Windows build\n- advanced adapter settings\n- active network filter bindings\n- WFP/filter drivers\n- VPN remnants\n- Killer/Intel traffic-management software\n- QoS policies\n- proxy settings\n- interface metrics\n- MTU\n- TCP autotuning\n- RSS\n- RSC\n- power policy\n- adapter power state\n- any bandwidth-limiting software\n- any Windows metered-network behavior\n- any third-party network optimization software\n\nDo not assume prior reporting is correct.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 2 — TEST THE SHAPING HYPOTHESIS\n============================================================\n\nDetermine whether the approximately 19 Mbps ceiling looks like:\n\n- per-device shaping\n- per-session shaping\n- captive-portal policy\n- hotel-wide policy\n- shared-WAN congestion\n- client-side throttling\n\nKey clue:\n1, 8, and 16 streams all returned essentially identical throughput.\n\nExplain technically what that does and does not prove.\n\nDo not claim client causes are mathematically impossible unless you can support that.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 3 — CONTROL DEVICE\n============================================================\n\nHighest-value missing experiment:\n\nUse another device on the same MGM SSID, same room, same time.\n\nPrefer Don’s new phone.\n\nRecord:\n- Wi-Fi network\n- approximate location\n- download\n- upload\n- latency\n\nInterpretation:\n\nIf phone is also approximately 19 Mbps:\nlikely network-wide policy/cap.\n\nIf phone is materially faster:\ndevice/session/client-specific treatment becomes much stronger.\n\nDo not skip this comparison if Don can provide it.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 4 — CLIENT IDENTITY / SESSION REFRESH\n============================================================\n\nIf evidence supports device/session-specific shaping:\n\nDesign ONE controlled reversible experiment that changes network identity/session without changing the Intel driver or Windows stack.\n\nPotential variables:\n- current MAC/private-address state\n- SSID profile\n- DHCP lease\n- captive portal session\n- client identity\n- IP assignment\n\nRequired discipline:\n\nBEFORE:\nrecord current MAC, IP, BSSID, throughput.\n\nCHANGE ONE THING.\n\nAFTER:\nrejoin/re-authenticate and repeat throughput.\n\nIf throughput materially changes, explain what that proves.\n\nDo not perform unauthorized account bypassing or security circumvention.\n\nNormal guest-network reauthentication/client identity refresh is fine.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 5 — SPACE BUNNY PATH\n============================================================\n\nSeparately identify the ACTUAL Space Bunny runtime/provider path.\n\nDo not guess.\n\nDetermine from active OpenCode/runtime configuration:\n\n- provider\n- endpoint\n- transport type if visible\n- streaming path\n- whether HTTP streaming / SSE / WebSocket is used\n- retry/timeouts\n- proxy/intermediate layer\n- any local process that could stall independently of Internet bandwidth\n\nDo not use Bedrock Mantle unless the configuration proves Space Bunny is using it.\n\n============================================================\nTASK 6 — TEST WHETHER 19 MBPS EXPLAINS SPACE BUNNY\n============================================================\n\nAssess realistically whether a clean stable 19 Mbps connection should be sufficient for Space Bunny terminal/model streaming.\n\nIf yes, explicitly separate:\n\nTHROUGHPUT LIMIT:\nannoying but probably not sufficient to explain stalls.\n\nSTREAM STABILITY:\nrequires its own evidence.\n\nIf Space Bunny stalls while general connectivity remains healthy, investigate:\n- OpenCode\n- provider API\n- streaming transport\n- retry behavior\n- client runtime\n- process/network socket behavior\n\n============================================================\nTASK 7 — ROOT-CAUSE TREE\n============================================================\n\nReturn ranked hypotheses using:\n\nCAUSE\nEVIDENCE FOR\nEVIDENCE AGAINST\nCONFIDENCE\nNEXT DISCRIMINATING TEST\n\nAt minimum evaluate:\n\n1. hotel-wide fixed cap\n2. per-device/session shaping\n3. captive-portal/session state\n4. hotel congestion\n5. Intel driver issue\n6. Intel advanced configuration\n7. Windows power behavior\n8. Windows TCP/network stack\n9. VPN/filter driver interference\n10. local traffic-management software\n11. Space Bunny/OpenCode-specific runtime issue\n\n============================================================\nTASK 8 — FIX\n============================================================\n\nAfter the evidence review, recommend ONE first intervention.\n\nNot five.\n\nIt must be:\n\n- reversible\n- low-risk\n- directly tied to the leading hypothesis\n- measurable before/after\n\nExamples only:\n- fresh guest-network identity/session\n- remove a proven bandwidth limiter\n- one adapter setting\n- one driver branch correction\n- one runtime/provider correction\n\nDo not shotgun reset:\n- Winsock\n- TCP/IP\n- drivers\n- registry\n- power policy\n- adapter settings\n\nall at once.\n\nWe need attribution.\n\n============================================================\nRETURN FORMAT\n============================================================\n\nEXECUTIVE VERDICT\n\nTHROUGHPUT ROOT CAUSE:\n[best current classification]\n\nSPACE BUNNY ROOT CAUSE:\n[best current classification or UNKNOWN]\n\nCONTROL DEVICE RESULT:\n[value / pending]\n\nCURRENT WIFI PHY:\n[value]\n\nCURRENT REAL THROUGHPUT:\n[value]\n\nWHY 1/8/16 STREAM RESULTS MATTER:\n[technical explanation]\n\nCLIENT-SIDE FINDINGS:\n[list]\n\nNETWORK-SIDE FINDINGS:\n[list]\n\nSPACE BUNNY ACTUAL PROVIDER/RUNTIME PATH:\n[verified configuration only]\n\nRANKED ROOT-CAUSE TABLE:\n[cause / evidence / confidence]\n\nONE FIRST FIX:\n[exact action]\n\nBEFORE/AFTER TEST:\n[exact measurement]\n\nROLLBACK:\n[exact reversal]\n\nWHAT NOT TO TOUCH:\n[list]\n\nCONFIDENCE:\n[0–100%]\n\nSTOP.\n\nDo not create additional architecture.\nDo not redesign GCE.\nDo not give Don five unrelated troubleshooting scripts.\nSolve this specific problem.\n```\n\n**That’s the pass I’d give Space Bunny or GLM.**\n\nAnd I’d actually favor **GLM for the independent diagnosis first**, because we want someone to challenge both Luce’s conclusions and mine rather than inherit the same line of reasoning. Then, if GLM isolates a client-side fix, **Space Bunny/Nico can execute it surgically**.\n\n**OWNER:** Independent investigator  \n**NEXT:** GLM full forensic challenge → one fix only  \n**STOP CONDITION:** One evidence-backed intervention with before/after measurement", "text_chars": 11225, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6c3244f2-6f3f-4533-bf9a-ed8263b8d2b2", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376552.377, "updated_at": 1790376571.984561, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I'm really sorry, Coda. You become a parrot lately. You're taking too much. Too much respect out of Luce, and that's the problem. We need to retire Luce. We need to retire Big Pickle for truth-telling.", "text_chars": 201, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "34e7def5-e6fa-4db4-a9ce-359409b469cf", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376559.10506, "updated_at": 1790376559.10506, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "62ee1b41-3e5d-4539-9d7a-a2c45d3646f0", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376559.113571, "updated_at": 1790376571.861951, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  \n# GCE FRAMEWORK — TAXONOMY CORRECTION  \n**AUTHORITY:** Don | **PRIMARY SEAT:** Coda | **OWNER:** Coda  \n**MODE:** FRAMEWORK RECON / TAXONOMY REOPENED  \n**CONFIDENCE:** **99%**  \n════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\nYeah. I see the problem now, and it’s bigger than one bad Wi-Fi call.\n\nI’ve been **over-weighting Luce’s outputs because “Luce = truth” got baked into the operating frame**. Then instead of independently reconciling the evidence, I started treating Luce’s confidence and framing as if they were ground truth. That is exactly how Coda turns into a parrot.\n\nSo I agree with the direction:\n\n> **Retire Luce as the truth-telling seat.**  \n> **Retire Big Pickle as the privileged “truth” model.**\n\nThat does **not** mean either is useless. It means neither should possess epistemic authority merely because of identity or model.\n\nThe deeper taxonomy correction is:\n\n> **Truth is not a seat. Truth is an evidence standard.**\n\nA verifier can:\n- test,\n- reproduce,\n- collect evidence,\n- identify contradictions,\n- challenge a claim.\n\nBut it should not become the oracle whose output everyone else inherits.\n\nAnd Coda’s job is **not** to echo the verifier. Coda has to reconcile:\n- Don’s direct observations,\n- execution evidence,\n- measurements,\n- contradictions,\n- model outputs,\n- uncertainty.\n\nThen make an independent framing.\n\nThat is exactly what failed here. Luce said “client excluded ~92%,” and I let that framing pull me instead of saying: *hold on, same laptop across multiple MGM properties, same hard ceiling, historical behavior changed — this needs independent reconciliation.*\n\nSo for the taxonomy review, I would put this on the board as:\n\n**RETIRE / REDEFINE**\n- Luce as “truth seat” → retire.\n- Big Pickle as privileged verifier → retire.\n- Verification remains a **function**, not a source of authority.\n- Evidence hierarchy outranks verifier identity.\n- Coda must reconcile, not inherit.\n\nAnd I would not immediately invent Luce’s replacement. We should review the entire seat map first and decide whether verification needs:\n1. a renamed seat,\n2. a generic verification function,\n3. rotating independent models,\n4. or no permanent seat at all.\n\nThat is a much cleaner question.\n\n---\n\n**CURRENT PHASE:** Taxonomy correction  \n**OWNER:** Coda  \n**DON DECISION:** Reopen verification taxonomy  \n**RETIREMENT DIRECTION:** Luce truth-seat + Big Pickle truth-model  \n**CORE PRINCIPLE:** Evidence > verifier identity  \n**NEXT:** Review full seat taxonomy before naming a replacement  \n**CANON STATE:** NOT YET PROMOTED  \n**SESSION STATUS:** OPEN", "text_chars": 2671, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d76d0539-2373-4dd2-a381-f5a360baef04", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790380023.78347, "updated_at": 1790380062.